/* Source-page English is first-pass, only partially literal and not all-register complete. */
window.GNAMMA_SANANDAJI_I=(window.GNAMMA_SANANDAJI_I||[]).concat([{"page":56,"body":"— What exactly is the contradictory of existence?\n*Original PDF physical page 69. Upper: connected first pass, ending at a page break; lower: selected response to the mediation claim.*\n\n### Upper register\n\n[The comparison includes] occurrence, unity, plurality, and things of that kind. **“Or [one may reply]”** by choosing the alternative that it is nonexistent: in this case it is not qualified by the contradictory [in the opponent's intended sense], but by *nonexistence*. For **“its contradictory is nonexistence (*ʿadam*), not the nonexistent (*maʿdūm*).”** If, however, *being qualified* is intended in its derivative, grammatical sense, [the writer reopens the difficulty]: every description which subsists in something is an individual [instance of that description]. The following words complete this objection on I:57.\n\n**Arabic checkpoints:** «والحدوث والوحدة والكثرة إلى غير ذلك» · «قد يجاب باختيار أنه معدوم» · «ولا اتصاف بالنقيض بل بالمعدوم» · «نقيضه العدم لا المعدوم» · «ولو أريد الاتصاف بطريق الاشتقاق».\n\n### Unassigned lower register — selected connected passage\n\nThe lower writer observes that, according to those who affirm an intermediary, **nonexistence does not simply equal the unrestricted negation of existence**. They introduce an intermediary in addition to existence and nonexistence. In their usage, therefore, nonexistence may be made more restricted than *non-existence* in the unrestricted contradictory sense. If everything that failed to be existent were immediately classed as nonexistent, there would be no room for the proposed intermediary at all.\n\nA difficulty follows. If the intermediary is itself neither existent nor nonexistent, what is the status of the statement by which one qualifies it? Calling it neither one nor the other will seem to require that the familiar contradiction has somehow been escaped. But if the qualification is said to be only conceptual, the opponent can ask whether the intermediary is thereby reduced to a consideration in the intellect rather than a distinct established category.\n\nThe gloss records the answer that *being qualified* may be used for what the intellect judges or attributes, even when there is no independently existent accident inhering in a subject. This move requires an account of what the predicate signifies and cannot be accepted as a second external entity merely because it is stated in a proposition. [The closing reply is incomplete in the source reading.]\n\n**Untranslated / uncertain:** Remaining lower refutations, including the discussion of whether the two meanings of *negation* have been held constant.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":57,"body":"— Are nonexistents distinguishable? Does possibility imply establishment?\n*Original PDF physical page 70. Upper: connected first pass; lower: two visibly distinct streams, one short and one extended.*\n\n### Upper register\n\n[The argument involves] particulars of its contradictory, just as blackness inhering in a body is one of the descriptions of that body. The body's being qualified by blackness does not entail that the body is itself blackness or that it is a non-body. Thus there is nothing strange, on the opponent's reading, in a particular way of speaking which describes existence as nonexistent [*in another respect*].\n\n**“They said”**—that is, those who count the nonexistent as a *thing* and as *established*—**“nonexistent things are distinguishable from one another.”** Some are objects of power, others are not; some may be represented and conceived, while others cannot be brought into the intended kind of representation. **“And differentiation cannot be intelligible without establishment.”** That is their inference.\n\nThey also maintain that **“every possible thing is established, because possibility is an affirmative attribute.”** A possible thing is described as possible, and what receives such a description must, they argue, have establishment. The distinction between the possibility belonging to something and its not possessing possibility leads into the next leaf. [End of this printed upper span.]\n\n**Arabic checkpoints:** «المعدومات متمايزة» · «بعضها عن بعض» · «بعضها مقدور» · «وبعضها غير المقدور» · «ولا يعقل التميز دون الثبوت» · «وكل ممكن ثابت لأن الإمكان وصف ثبوتي».\n\n### Unassigned lower register — first, shorter ruled note\n\nOn the comparison with blackness and a body: **being qualified by nonexistence would imply that the existent in question was not existent**. But being qualified by blackness does not make a body a different sort of thing, still less reduce it to a non-body. Thus the analogy cannot simply be admitted; the alleged cases of qualification are not of the same form. [The note closes with an abbreviation but no positive identification of its writer.]\n\n### Unassigned lower register — opening of the longer note\n\nOn **“that the body is a non-body”**: the glossator asks whether the example is intended to deny a body's own corporeal reality or merely to say that a description can be negated of a subject without denying the subject itself. The first would be inadmissible. But, he continues, it does not follow that every expression “existence is qualified by nonexistence” should be construed as the existence of a contrary accident. What the negation is applied to, and what precisely is taken to be negated, must be distinguished. The writer refers to a more extended earlier treatment and proceeds to the argument about possible things on I:58.\n\n**Untranslated / uncertain:** Middle of the extended note on the different senses of *maʿdūm* and the rebuttal to the claim that possible nonexistents are established.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":58,"body":"— Possibility as an affirmative description\n*Original PDF physical page 71. Upper: first-pass translation; long lower reply only selectively translated.*\n\n### Upper register\n\n[The distinction at issue concerns] the possible *in itself* in relation to a given thing. A version of the *Talḵīṣ* is cited for the expression **“the distinction between the establishment of its possibility and its not having possibility.”** The point is that there can be a difference between possibility being established for a thing and possibility being denied of it. [The exact scope of the negation in the printed expression requires reinspection; this is not used to supply an additional argument.]\n\nIt does not follow that, for a possible thing to be possible, possibility itself must be an independently existent entity in the possible thing. The challenge turns upon whether this possibility is being treated as an affirmative attribute and what such affirmation requires. **“Its subject is established”** is the opponent's conclusion; for, they say, an affirmative description of what is not established would require the subject to have some kind of existence or establishment. This is precisely the inference contested here, and the discussion continues on I:59.\n\n**Arabic checkpoints:** «الفرق بين ثبوت الإمكان ... ولا إمكان له» · «الإمكان ... ثبوتيا» · «ثبت موصوفه» · «لأن اتصاف غير الثابت بالثابت».\n\n### Unassigned lower register — provisional rendering of an objection\n\nThe marginal writer proposes a distinction between the intellect's affirming *that something is possible* and the supposed external subsistence of possibility as an attribute added to that thing. To affirm its possibility is not yet to make possibility itself an independently established item. The contested claim is whether an affirmation about a nonexistent subject entails the establishment of that subject **in itself**, or merely its availability as a subject of a judgment.\n\nThe note further questions whether the difference between “possibility is established for it” and “there is no possibility for it” is a difference between two existing attributes. The contradiction can be understood as a difference between what judgments the intellect may make; it need not introduce another external intermediary. [The author's remaining distinctions concerning the object of possibility and its contradictory are not fully translated.]","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":59,"body":"— Differentiation, contradiction, and what exists only in thought\n*Original PDF physical page 72. Upper: first-pass passage; lower: selected notes.*\n\n### Upper register\n\n**“We say”**: if by this you intend that differentiation entails **external** establishment, we deny it. That consequence would follow only if the differentiation in question were itself external [in the required sense]. The fact that intellect distinguishes objects does not by itself establish the distinguished items outside the intellect.\n\nKnow that there are also descriptions involving mutually incompatible conditions—for example, the assembling of contradictories or a body's occupying two different places at one and the same instant. Such descriptions can be distinguished from each other and from existent things, although the combinations described are decisively impossible. If their distinguishability entailed external existence, the impossible would have to be externally realized.\n\nThe objection from **“imaginary composites”** can likewise be answered: their being distinguished in the mind is not a proof that they possess the extramental existence which the argument seeks. [The discussion then returns to the possible and its possibility.]\n\n**“The distinction between the establishment of possibility for a thing and possibility in itself”** is a difference found by considering the proposition. On the supposition of the thing as nonexistent, the possibility is not established *in the external world* as another item simply because reason can consider it. The relevant assertion is made in intellectual consideration. [The final clause continues on I:60.]\n\n**Arabic checkpoints:** «قلنا إن أردت أن التميز يقتضي الثبوت في الخارج فمنعوا» · «واجتماع النقيضين» · «وكون الجسم في آن واحد في حيزين» · «والتركيبات الخيالية» · «والفرق بين ثبوت الإمكان عن شيء» · «غير ثابت في الخارج أصلا بمجرد اعتبار العقل».\n\n### Unassigned lower register — selected rebuttal\n\nOn **“it does not follow”**: the writer notes that the subject is the absolutely nonexistent, which has no external existence, not a thing that merely happens to be absent from the current situation while existing elsewhere. To make the object possible in intellectual consideration is therefore not to grant the external existence which was denied at the outset.\n\nOn **“in itself”**: if the possibility is not an established attribute inhering in an independently existent subject, it is misleading to infer a second establishment for it. The dispute partly turns on whether one is using *in itself* to mean real actuality or the difference between what can and cannot be coherently conceived. [The remainder of the lower reply is not treated as fully read.]","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":60,"body":"— Predication, external qualification, and contradictory conditions\n*Original PDF physical page 73. Upper: first-pass translation; lower: selected argument.*\n\n### Upper register\n\n[If the possibility] is taken as an attribute [of something in itself], the claimed consequence still does not follow merely from its negation. Your statement that **“the establishment of something for something presupposes the establishment of the thing itself”** must be qualified. It is true, for example, of the occurrence of a quality in an external subject: as whiteness belongs to a body in the external world, the subject has to be there for that mode of inherence to take place.\n\nBut the *predication* of a thing and the truth of a judgment concerning it are not invariably the same as a quality's inhering externally in an existent subject. If the discussion is about something absent or nonexistent, there may still be a meaningful judgment concerning what it would be were it to exist. The use of affirmation therefore cannot indiscriminately be transferred from external inherence to all meaningful propositions.\n\n**“Then all existence and nonexistence would become impossible”**: this is the absurd consequence alleged by the opponent. They argue that, if contrary descriptions are asserted without fixing the sense of their predication, existence and nonexistence will be gathered together, or the impossible will be described as possible. The reply insists that the judgments are made in different respects and that the disputed inferential step has not been established.\n\n**Arabic checkpoints:** «ثبوت شيء لشيء فرع ثبوته في نفسه» · «كبياض الجسم» · «والصدق عليه» · «اجتماع النقيضين ممتنع» · «الأوصاف الصادقة على التي بعضها ثبوتية وبعضها سلبية».\n\n### Unassigned lower register — translated selection\n\nA distinction must be made between a real affirmative determination, whose presence is being claimed in a subject, and a *negative* determination which rejects something without positing a further independently real accident. Calling something “nonexistent” or “not qualified in such-and-such a way” may be an instance of the second kind. The exclusion of an affirmative attribute is not itself an additional affirmative attribute of the same kind.\n\nThe gloss argues that this is the source of the contradiction alleged by the disputant: he has moved from a judgment in which absence is denied or a qualification is withheld to one in which the contrary is supposed to exist as a positive attribute. Such a move must be justified. [A long lower passage comparing possible, impossible and contrary propositions remains untranslated.]","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":61,"body":"— Identity of subject and predicate: the conditions of informative judgment\n*Original PDF physical page 74. Upper: unusually long, connected first pass; lower: selected argument.*\n\n### Upper register\n\n[The judgment requires] **the union of the two terms in the actual thing (*dhātan wa-huwiyyatan*)**: that what the subject-expression applies to outside [the mind], and what is denoted by the predicate-expression, are the same thing in the respect asserted. Otherwise the judgment “this is that” would not be valid, since one already knows that it does not hold of things that are entirely different in their individual identities.\n\nBut there must also be **“a difference between them in meaning.”** That is, the concept signified by the subject-expression must differ from the concept signified by the predicate-expression, so that the judgment conveys something: namely, that two descriptions different *as concepts* are united *in the thing*. One gains nothing by saying merely “the earth is earth” or “the sky is sky.” Those sentences assert what is already contained in the way the subject is presented.\n\nOne may answer that, although a subject cannot fail to be either qualified by the predicate or qualified by its contradictory, the person who conceives the subject does not thereby know *which* of the two holds. It is therefore not a contradiction to need a further judgment, nor must the predication be useless. A proposition may be **true**, corresponding to the thing, or **false** and invalid.\n\nThe truth of a judgment is not simply its correspondence to what is externally present, since its two terms may fail to be realized externally. Nor is it correspondence to whatever happens to be represented in the mind; the intellect may represent false judgments as well. Rather, the truth of a judgment is its correspondence to **what is the case in itself** (*mā fī nafs al-amr*). This expression is explained in the next leaf.\n\n**Arabic checkpoints:** «الجهل الإيجابي إلى اتحاد الطرفين ذاتا وهوية» [reading of the introductory wording uncertain] · «تغايرهما مفهوما» · «أن هذين المتغايرين بحسب المفهوم متحدان بحسب الذات» · «الأرض أرض والسماء سماء» · «قد تكون صحيحة أي صدقا خارجيا وقد تكون فاسدة أي كذبا وباطلا» · «ليس صدق الحكم وصحته مطابقته إلى الأعيان».\n\n### Unassigned lower register — provisional rendering of selected distinction\n\nIf the subject and predicate are said to be united in external existence, that does not by itself mean that their *concepts* are identical. The intellect may form a conception of the subject under one description and then learn something else by predicating a distinct description of it. Conversely, a mere diversity of phrases does not make a judgment genuinely informative unless the cognitions conveyed differ in the relevant way. The lower text separately considers the referents of the expressions and the way those referents are grasped in intellect. [This lower argument is not reproduced clause for clause.]","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":62,"body":"— The meaning of *nafs al-amr*\n*Original PDF physical page 75. Upper: short continuing conclusion; most of the leaf is a lengthy lower gloss.*\n\n### Upper register\n\n[Truth is] **correspondence to what is in the thing itself** (*mā fī nafs al-amr*). By *al-amr* is meant “the thing” and by *nafs* its “self” or essence. Its meaning is what one understands when saying, **“This matter is thus in itself”**: that is, as the thing stands in its own right, **“apart from the judgment of the one judging and the apprehension of the one apprehending.”**\n\n**Arabic checkpoints:** «مطابقته لما في نفس الأمر» · «والمراد بالأمر الشيء وبالنفس الذات» · «ومعناه ما فهم من قولنا هذا الأمر كذا في نفسه» · «مع قطع النظر عن حكم الحاكم وإدراك المدرك».\n\n### Unassigned lower register — selected opening of the extended note\n\nThe note questions how correspondence can be conceived in judgments whose subjects are not instantiated among external things—for example, judgments concerning merely possible, impossible, or universally considered objects. If correspondence were restricted to independently existent external objects, such judgments could not be true. The gloss therefore distinguishes **the state of affairs according to which a proposition is true** from the mere occurrence of an act of judging it or the occurrence of a particular form in someone's understanding.\n\nThe text explores whether a predicate's belonging to a subject in this sense has to be *in external existence*, or whether the correspondence may hold of an intelligible without the independently existing subject stipulated by the opponent. [The subsequent discussion of the precise meaning of self-identity and the alleged contradiction is not exhaustively translated.]\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":63,"body":"— A new chapter: what quiddity answers\n*Original PDF physical page 76. Authentic printed heading **فصل في الماهية**; brief upper opening, extensive lower commentary.*\n\n### Upper register — new printed chapter\n\n**“Chapter concerning quiddity”** (*faṣl fī l-māhiyya*). He places the inquiries into quiddity after those into existence and nonexistence, although the inquiry into existence and nonexistence presupposes something to which either can be ascribed, and therefore their consideration is posterior to quiddity in one respect. [The phrase explaining the order of presentation is compressed and requires further proof against the source.]\n\n**“Quiddity is that by which an answer is given to the question, ‘What is it?’”** The word *māhiyya* is connected with the interrogative *mā huwa* (“what is it?”), as when someone asks after the whatness of a thing. [The following example, beginning with quantity, continues on I:64.]\n\n**Arabic checkpoints:** «فصل في الماهية» · «أخّر مباحثها عن مباحث الوجود والعدم» · «الماهية ما به يجاب عن السؤال بما هو».\n\n### Unassigned lower commentary — directly translated opening (continuation and remainder outstanding)\n\nOn **“Chapter concerning quiddity,”** the writer reports the substance of what is said in the *Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid*: **When quiddity is considered together with its being actualized, it is called a *dhāt* and a *ḥaqīqa*.** Thus one does not say “the essence and reality of the phoenix” in precisely that actualizing sense. **When it is considered together with individuation, it is called a *huwiyya*.** The term *huwiyya* can also be applied to individuation itself or to external existence, and *dhāt* may be applied to individual instances of which a quiddity is predicated. **Moreover, a quiddity is accompanied by its accidents, whether inseparable or separable.** [End of the cited statement in the printed gloss.]\n\nThen comes a question: **If quiddity is included among general matters, is that not incorrect?** For the general matters are states and attributes that are not confined to one particular division of existence. Quiddity, by contrast, is being treated here as the answer to the question about what a given thing is. [The ensuing objection and reply about the different meanings of *māhiyya* extend over the rest of this long lower note; a complete literal translation has not yet been made.]\n\n**Checked Arabic in the lower note:** «قال في شرح المقاصد ما حاصله أن الماهية إذا اعتبرت مع التحقق سميت ذاتا وحقيقة» · «وإذا اعتبرت مع التشخص سميت هوية» · «وقد تطلق الهوية على التشخص أو الوجود الخارجي» · «وقد يطلق الذات على مصدقات الماهية من الأفراد» · «ثم إن الماهية تقارن جميع عوارضها لازمة أو مفارقة».","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":64,"body":"— Quiddity taken with an accompanying condition: *al-makhlūṭa*\n*Original PDF physical page 77. Upper: very short complete visible passage up to its final conjunction; lower: selected explanatory lines.*\n\n### Upper register\n\n[The preceding example concerns] what answers the question **“What is it?”** And the quiddity **“may be taken on condition of something”** (*bi-sharṭ shayʾ*): that is, on condition of its being accompanied by accidents. It is then **“called the mixed [quiddity]”** (*al-makhlūṭa*), because it is considered together with accidents. **“There is no obscurity in its existence”**: the existence of individual concrete things outside the mind is evident, as in Zayd and ʿAmr, who are individuals of humanity. **“And [it may be ...]”** [a second mode of consideration follows on I:65.]\n\n**Arabic checkpoints:** «قد تؤخذ بشرط شيء» · «أي المقارنة للعوارض» · «تسمى المخلوطة» · «لخلطها مع العوارض» · «ولا خفاء في وجودها» · «وجود الأشخاص في الخارج بين كزيد وعمرو من أفراد الإنسان».\n\n### Unassigned lower commentary — provisional interpretation of selected distinctions\n\nThe writer asks why the quiddity is divided in this fashion. A divided item should, it seems, be one of the members of the division; but the same quiddity can be considered with or without the accompanying conditions. The division therefore concerns **ways of considering one nature**, not necessarily three realities independent of one another.\n\nThe distinction can be applied both to the individual's additional properties and to attributes whose attribution follows the nature under a particular condition. The gloss observes that the question **“what is it?”** can be answered by something indicating the nature itself, whereas the individual named in the example has a quiddity accompanied by individuating characteristics. The latter are not for that reason parts of the definition of humanity. [The long subsequent dispute over the division's logical status is not translated in full.]","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":65,"body":"— Quiddity taken on condition of no accompaniment: *al-mujarrada*\n*Original PDF physical page 78. Upper: continuing first-pass translation; lower: opening technical distinction translated.*\n\n### Upper register\n\n[Quiddity may] **“be taken on condition that there is no thing [accompanying it]”** (*bi-sharṭ lā shayʾ*): that is, [on condition of] being free of accidents and concomitants. It is then **“called abstracted”** (*al-mujarrada*), because it has been abstracted from them. **“It has no existence in minds, let alone among external individuals.”** For existence in the mind, whether the accidents from which one abstracts are taken without restriction or restricted to **external** accidents, is itself among the accidents [that follow a quiddity]. Being in the mind is a determination belonging to the mental form; hence a quiddity stipulated to have no concomitant at all cannot, under that very stipulation, acquire mental existence. [The passage continues on I:66.]\n\n**Arabic checkpoints:** «تؤخذ بشرط لا شيء» · «أي الخلو عن العوارض واللواحق» · «تسمى المجردة» · «ولا وجود في الأذهان فضلا عن الأعيان» · «فإن الكون في الذهن من العوارض».\n\n### Unassigned lower commentary — directly translated opening, followed by an unverified remainder\n\nOn **“for existence is an accident”**: Know that they divide what is accidental to a thing into two divisions. **One is an accident of existence; the other is an accident of quiddity.** They also make existence [relevant here] twofold: one is what attaches to a thing **on condition of external existence**, such as blackness and whiteness; the second is what attaches to it **on condition of mental existence**, such as universality and particularity. Other concomitants of a quiddity are not all required without qualification, and their conditions of occurrence have to be considered. [The printed lower argument continues with a technical objection about which, if any, of the three ways of taking quiddity includes existence.]\n\n**Checked Arabic in the lower note:** «واعلم أنهم قسموا العارض لشيء قسمين أحدهما عارض الوجود والآخر عارض الماهية» · «وجعلوا الوجود أيضا قسمين» · «أحدهما ما يعرض لشيء بشرط الوجود الخارجي كالسواد والبياض» · «والثاني ما يعرض بشرط الوجود الذهني كالكلية والجزئية».\n\n**Untranslated lower remainder:** The lengthy continuation concerns absolute and restricted accidental determinations and whether *mental being* is itself a condition incompatible with quiddity taken *bi-sharṭ lā*. It has not been translated literally and is not included in this page's completed-text count.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":66,"body":"— Why the mental consideration does not qualify the nature in the stipulated respect\n*Original PDF physical page 79. Upper: short linked translation; lower: selected passage only.*\n\n### Upper register\n\n[One may be taking the nature] in the mind, while **not noticing or regarding that being-in-the-mind as an accompanying accident** of it. Nevertheless, that through which the nature is actually supposed to be present in the mind has mental existence as a determination in fact, even if the one considering the nature does not direct attention to that determination.\n\nAs for **judging that [the strictly abstracted nature's] existence is impossible**, a judgment cannot be made about something without first conceiving it. That requirement to conceive the object, however, does not entail that the object has the very kind of existence which the judgment declares impossible. It may be conceived in one respect and denied existence in another; the distinction between the act of considering and what the condition excludes must be preserved. [The statement leads into I:67.]\n\n**Arabic checkpoints:** «الذهن» · «كونه في الذهن من غير أن نعتبر الذهن عارضا له» · «وأما الحكم عليها باستحالة وجودها» · «والحكم على الشيء إلا بعد تصوره».\n\n### Unassigned lower commentary — selected argument\n\nOn **“that one may not treat it as an accident,”** the marginal writer distinguishes an object's being conceived from the intellect's **taking its own act of conceiving as part of the object conceived**. The latter would indeed give an additional condition; the former may occur without being explicitly noticed in the concept. Yet the act of conceiving does not abolish the actual mental circumstance under which the thought takes place.\n\nThis is why, according to the objection, *negating* mental existence of a quiddity in one respect must not be confused with denying that there is a mental cognition of it at all. If the very act of thinking were incompatible with the judgment, one could not even formulate the statement that the strictly abstracted quiddity is impossible under the specified condition. [The rest of the long lower dispute is not translated clause by clause.]","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":67,"body":"— Quiddity considered absolutely, without stipulating presence or absence of accidents\n*Original PDF physical page 80. Upper: connected passage; lower: selected objection.*\n\n### Upper register\n\n[Accordingly, the nature] **can be considered abstracted in respect of itself** and yet, **in respect of its existence in the mind**, fall under the category of the mixed nature. It is thus possible to judge that its existence is impossible **in the respect in which it is strictly abstracted**, without denying that the quiddity is conceived under another consideration.\n\n**“And [it] may be taken without stipulating anything”** (*lā bi-sharṭ shayʾ*)—neither stipulating its being accompanied by accidents nor stipulating its being abstracted from them. **“It is more general than the mixed [quiddity].”** The basis of the assertion is the greater extension of what is taken without this condition. The comparison is pursued on I:68.\n\n**Arabic checkpoints:** «من حيث ذاتها مجردة ومقارنة للمخلوطة» · «ومن حيث وجودها في الذهن قسما من المخلوطة» · «فيصح الحكم عليها باستحالة الوجود من حيث ذاتها» · «وقد تؤخذ لا بشرط شيء» · «وهي أعم من المخلوطة».\n\n### Unassigned lower commentary — selected passage\n\nA celebrated difficulty is invoked about the abstracted nature. If one judges it possible or impossible of existence, is one taking it without a condition when making the judgment, or attaching the very condition under dispute? The two considerations must not be transferred into each other.\n\nThe writer distinguishes the quiddity itself from the consideration by which the intellect separates it from accidents, and in turn from the mental determination attaching to the *act* of abstraction. The *non-conditioned* way of taking a nature neither asserts that its accidents accompany it nor asserts that they cannot accompany it. It therefore has a different logical scope from the nature explicitly conditioned by their absence. [The extensive discussion of the two conditions and their relative generality is not completely translated.]","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":68,"body":"— The unconditioned nature in external individuals\n*Original PDF physical page 81. Upper: connected first-pass translation, cut off at the physical page break; lower: provisional rendering of opening distinction.*\n\n### Upper register\n\n[The nature taken without a condition is more general than] the strictly abstracted nature, for it may be predicated of what is abstracted as well as of what is qualified or restricted. The unrestricted applies to a restricted instance; that is the point of the proposed comparison.\n\n**“It exists”**—the quiddity taken *without a condition* exists **“in the external world, because it is the very [nature of] the mixed [quiddity] there,”** **“not a part of it.”** The difference is not that two externally subsisting natures have been joined together. Rather, the unconditioned quiddity is predicated of the nature in its concrete, accompanied condition without, in the concept itself, requiring the accompanying determinations.\n\n**“For the human who is not [externally present] as an individual”**: one must distinguish the unconditioned humanity of which the intellect forms a concept from Zayd or ʿAmr, the concrete individuals of whom it is predicated. Existence in the external world belongs to the individual; yet the nature predicated of the individual need not be taken as one separable *piece* alongside the individuating accompaniments. The proposition does not require an isolated pure humanity to subsist independently in the external world. [The last upper clause extends onto I:69.]\n\n**Arabic checkpoints:** «المجردة لصدقها على ما هو مجرد» · «وصدق المطلق على المقيد» · «توجد الماهية لا بشرط في الخارج» · «لكونها نفسها» · «أي نفس المخلوطة في الخارج» · «لا جزءا منها» · «الإنسان الموجود من الإنسان والأفراد» [last reading provisional].\n\n### Unassigned lower commentary — source-based opening translation\n\nOn **“because it is the very nature of the mixed [quiddity]”**, the gloss notes that the unconditioned nature should not be described simply as an externally separable part of the particular, since the particular consists of its nature together with what individuates it, not of two independently existing objects. The statement “humanity exists outside [the mind]” is true only when the reference to its actual occurrence in individual human beings is properly understood.\n\nThe writer asks whether “absolute humanity” names the nature abstracted from all accidents, or the nature considered *without* making either their presence or absence a condition. If the first meaning is intended, it would be a mistake to infer the existence of the strictly abstracted nature externally. If the second is intended, the existence of individual humans can be the basis for predicating the nature of them. The remaining lower argument is not yet translated fully.\n\n---\n\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":69,"body":"— The natural universal and the distinction between the nature and its mental predicate\n\n*Original printed Vol. I:69 · primary PDF physical page 82 · source-inspected first-pass text · not independently proofread.*\n\n### Upper printed commentary (Taftāzānī lemmata + Sanandajī, mixed)\n\n[Continuing the discussion of what is predicated of an external individual:] The difference between the unrestricted and the restricted is [a difference] in the intellect, not something requiring two externally separate existents. **“Then, when [the nature] taken without a condition is considered as receiving the universal”**—that is, the logical universal—**“it is the natural universal.”** What exists of this in the external world is the *subject* to which universality is ascribed, namely the quiddity which is the subject of that attribution, **not the universal as an attribute** added to it. For the subject's external existence is accompanied by individuation.\n\nA question arises: should one not then deny external existence of the subject altogether, since the universality ascribed to it is not externally present? The answer is that the subject does not exist *abstracted from accidents*: among these are universality, genericity, specificity, and similar descriptions, which as such are mental attributes and preclude the stipulated abstracted condition. Consequently, when it is said that the *natural universal exists externally*, the intended meaning requires further explanation: it concerns what the universal is predicated of, not the external existence of universality considered as a mental qualification. [This sentence is completed on I:70.]\n\n### Original Arabic anchors (not full transcription)\n\nالكلي الطبيعي ؛ وإنما يوجد منه المعروض ؛ دون الكلي العارض ؛ مجردا عن العوارض\n\n### Separate lower-register commentary — selected source renderings and interpretive explanations, author unassigned\n\nOn the commentary's phrase “in the intellect, not in the external world,” the marginal writer distinguishes difference **in concept** from difference **in what a predicate is true of**. He asks whether “the mixed quiddity” denotes the qualified quiddity itself or the whole of that quiddity and its individuating accompaniments. If the second meaning is intended, then calling the unconditioned nature a *part* of the individual is different from saying that it is identical with the quiddity predicated of that individual. The sense in which “human” is predicated of Zayd cannot be decided simply by treating every accompanying determination as another independently existing part. [The remainder of the objection continues across I:70 and has not been fully translated.]\n\n**Source-and-completeness caution:** The lower printed passage is longer than the selected English here; unresolved clauses and page joins are not silently filled. It remains a separately authored stream and is not a completed ḥāshiya translation.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":70,"body":"— Only the particularized nature exists outside; the intellectual universal does not\n\n*Original printed Vol. I:70 · primary PDF physical page 83 · source-inspected first-pass text · not independently proofread.*\n\n### Upper printed commentary (Taftāzānī lemmata + Sanandajī, mixed)\n\nWhat the [logical universal] is predicated of—the nature taken without a condition—possesses existence in the external world, but that existence is attained only when individuation occurs. Therefore what is actually existent is **the mixed [quiddity], “that is, the individuals.”** The feminine pronoun in the cited wording is explained either by regard to its predicate or by reference to the quiddity under consideration.\n\nBy contrast, the nature considered **together with the accident of universality** does not exist as such externally. Neither does the compound of the nature and this mental accident—that is, the *intellectual universal*—exist externally. The explanation so far of the quiddity taken *on condition of the absence of anything* is the familiar account. **“It has also been said”**—the writer identifies Ibn Sīnā as the proponent—**“that the quiddity taken on condition of ...”** [The alternative terminological explanation continues on I:71.]\n\n### Original Arabic anchors (not full transcription)\n\nفالوجود حقيقة هي المخلوطة ؛ وهي الأفراد ؛ وأما المأخوذ مع عارض الكلية فلا توجد ؛ الكلي العقلي ؛ والقائل ابن سينا\n\n### Separate lower-register commentary — selected source renderings and interpretive explanations, author unassigned\n\nThe marginal gloss summarizes the difference: the mere quiddity taken without a condition is not the logical universal, *except insofar as it is considered to be the subject of that universal*. What becomes present externally is the nature without the mental qualification of universality, and it is present in circumstances involving accidents particular to individuals. The quiddity accompanied by the **mental accident** of being universal has no such extramental existence. This also explains why the gloss insists on separating what has a predicate from that predicate itself. [Other distinctions made here regarding the sense of “individual” remain to be collated.]\n\n**Source-and-completeness caution:** The lower printed passage is longer than the selected English here; unresolved clauses and page joins are not silently filled. It remains a separately authored stream and is not a completed ḥāshiya translation.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":71,"body":"— Ibn Sīnā’s alternative: matter, genus, and the three conditional considerations\n\n*Original printed Vol. I:71 · primary PDF physical page 84 · source-inspected first-pass text · not independently proofread.*\n\n### Upper printed commentary (Taftāzānī lemmata + Sanandajī, mixed)\n\n**“On condition of no thing”** means, in this alternative account, that what accompanies a quiddity is considered *additional to it*: the accompanying matters are taken as outside the meaning of the quiddity. **“Then it is matter”**—a part of **“the individual”**, understood as the composite of the quiddity and what accompanies it—and **“prior to it in both modes of existence,”** extramental and mental. For a whole cannot be realized without its part. The priority intended is natural priority, not a claim that the material part exists earlier in time. On this consideration the quiddity cannot be predicated of the whole, since the condition of predicability, unity in existence, has been excluded.\n\nFor example, if *animal* is taken on condition that the rational [differentia] is not included with it, it is a part and matter for the compound of animality and rationality, and it is not predicated of that compound. If it is taken on condition that the rational is included with it, it is a *species*. If it is taken without stipulating whether something accompanies it, two considerations are possible: one may attend to its difference from what accompanies it, or to their unity. Accordingly, animal taken in the first sense is a part of the human being, prior to him in mental and extramental being, and is not predicated of him. [The corresponding species and genus cases follow on I:72.]\n\n### Original Arabic anchors (not full transcription)\n\nالماهية بشرط لا شيء ؛ فتكون مادة ؛ للشخص ؛ متقدمة عليه في الوجودين ؛ التقدم بالطبع ؛ الحيوان ؛ الناطق\n\n### Separate lower-register commentary — selected source renderings and interpretive explanations, author unassigned\n\nA short, separately ruled marginal note warns that whether the *whole* is itself externally existent has been disputed, though Ibn Sīnā and the account cited here treat a real compound as having a unity. Another marginal argument asks whether *animality* taken without rationality is literally a material constituent of an externally existing human being, or a prior item in intellectual analysis. On the first reading, predication of animality of the man is problematic; on the second, the “part” is a conceptual constituent. The note also presses the question of whether a genus can be genuinely predicated when its accompanying differentia is excluded by stipulation. [The lengthy ensuing qualifications have not been translated in full.]\n\n**Source-and-completeness caution:** The lower printed passage is longer than the selected English here; unresolved clauses and page joins are not silently filled. It remains a separately authored stream and is not a completed ḥāshiya translation.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":72,"body":"— Animal as matter or species; a marginal discussion of the Avicennian account\n\n*Original printed Vol. I:72 · primary PDF physical page 85 · source-inspected first-pass text · not independently proofread.*\n\n### Upper printed commentary (Taftāzānī lemmata + Sanandajī, mixed)\n\n[Continuing the example:] **“The second”**—animality taken with the relevant condition—**“is the very human being; and the third is a genus predicated of it.”** Then [the cited text turns to] **“the simple quiddity.”** [This is almost the entirety of the upper register on this leaf. Its extended lower argument must be consulted separately.]\n\n### Original Arabic anchors (not full transcription)\n\nوالثاني نفس الإنسان ؛ والثالث جنس محمول عليه ؛ ثم الماهية البسيطة\n\n### Separate lower-register commentary — selected source renderings and interpretive explanations, author unassigned\n\nThe marginal writer cites the explanation that the quiddity taken *without a condition* can be considered both in its difference from what accompanies it and in its unity with that accompaniment. In the first respect it can be treated as an intrinsic constituent; in the second, it may be predicated of the whole. But the gloss objects that this statement does not simply agree with the alternative account as it is expressed in another commentary.\n\nThe writer then returns to the fuller explanation attributed to Ibn Sīnā. A nature can be taken so that, if anything accompanies it, that accompaniment is regarded as outside its meaning. In such a case it cannot be predicated of the composite containing that accompaniment; it constitutes a part or *matter* of the composite. Conversely, the same nature can be taken together with an accompaniment as included in what is meant, and then the predication concerns the constituted whole. A third consideration is possible in which neither the inclusion nor the exclusion of a concomitant is required. Such a nature is able to receive further determination and may function as a genus. [This is a selected connected translation from the marginal argument on I:72–73, not its complete wording.]\n\n**Source-and-completeness caution:** The lower printed passage is longer than the selected English here; unresolved clauses and page joins are not silently filled. It remains a separately authored stream and is not a completed ḥāshiya translation.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":73,"body":"— Continuation of the lower gloss: genus and species as ways of taking one nature\n\n*Original printed Vol. I:73 · primary PDF physical page 86 · source-inspected first-pass text · not independently proofread.*\n\n### Upper printed text\n\nThere is **no new upper printed passage** at this original page position; the page consists of a continuing lower gloss.\n\n### Original Arabic anchors (not full transcription)\n\n*None: no new upper text on this page.*\n\n### Separate lower-register commentary — selected source renderings and interpretive explanations, author unassigned\n\nThe text attributed to Ibn Sīnā's *Shifāʾ* is said to have been abridged by the investigator in the commentary on the *Ishārāt*. The glossator warns that the familiar threefold distinction, as taught by later scholars, must not simply be substituted for the particular considerations expressed in that account.\n\nIf *animal* is taken in the first manner, its nature is taken independently of the rational differentia, so that animality is part of the human compound and is not predicated of that compound under the excluding condition. In the second manner, animality is taken together with rationality in the constituted whole, and what is spoken of is the human species. In the third manner, the meaning is not confined by either restriction; it is capable of being predicated of the human being and of other species that share animality. In that respect it is a genus.\n\nOn this terminology, what is called the *mixed quiddity*, taken with an accompanying condition, is the compound of the quiddity and what accompanies it and thus coincides with the existing individuals. The nature taken without stipulation is open to receiving the determinations of more than one kind; viewed in that way, it is neither simply the whole nor simply a separately subsisting piece of it. The glossator criticizes the easy assimilation of genus, differentia and species to one another merely because they are in a certain respect one in being. Their predicability and their place in the logical constitution must still be distinguished. [The lower page continues with further criticisms of the account and with the question of universal predication; these have not been translated completely.]\n\n**Source-and-completeness caution:** The lower printed passage is longer than the selected English here; unresolved clauses and page joins are not silently filled. It remains a separately authored stream and is not a completed ḥāshiya translation.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":74,"body":"— Why a compound must ultimately contain uncombined constituents\n\n*Original printed Vol. I:74 · primary PDF physical page 87 · source-inspected first-pass text · not independently proofread.*\n\n### Upper printed commentary (Taftāzānī lemmata + Sanandajī, mixed)\n\n[Continuing the discussion of simple quiddity:] It has no parts, as [the point does not], while what is composed from it, such as a body, has a composition. **“For the absence of composite quiddities”** would entail the absence of **simple** ones. **“But it does not follow that [the series of quiddities] ends in the simple.”** For unity is a principle of multiplicity: just as one cannot have a number, whether finite or infinite, without units, so one cannot have what is multiple without there being in it some unities—that is, items not actually divided, whether or not they are capable of division.\n\nComposition may be **real**: a single reality results from a plurality of things and possesses its own characteristics, concomitants and effects. Or it may be **conceptual**: the intellect regards a number of things as one although there is no such single thing in the extramental world. **“The dependence of some parts upon others in a real compound is necessary.”** For if each part were completely independent of the other, a single real quiddity would not result from them. [The need for parts is further analysed on I:75.]\n\n### Original Arabic anchors (not full transcription)\n\nوانتهائها إلى البسيطة ؛ الواحد مبدأ التعدد ؛ التركيب قد يكون حقيقيا ؛ وقد يكون اعتباريا ؛ واحتياج بعض الأجزاء إلى بعض في المركب الحقيقي ضروري\n\n### Separate lower-register commentary — selected source renderings and interpretive explanations, author unassigned\n\nThe lower commentator questions a too-quick inference from the existence of a composed reality to the existence of a simple component *as a separately subsisting entity*. A real whole is indeed conceived through its constituents, but it may be an additional mistake to assume that each of those constituents must exist independently in the manner of a finished individual. The explanatory issue is whether the dependence of a whole upon its components is physical, essential, or solely the product of intellectual analysis. [The lengthy lower development, including the discussion of the one and number, remains untranslated in full.]\n\n**Source-and-completeness caution:** The lower printed passage is longer than the selected English here; unresolved clauses and page joins are not silently filled. It remains a separately authored stream and is not a completed ḥāshiya translation.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":75,"body":"— Real and conceptual compounds; matter and form\n\n*Original printed Vol. I:75 · primary PDF physical page 88 · source-inspected first-pass text · not independently proofread.*\n\n### Upper printed commentary (Taftāzānī lemmata + Sanandajī, mixed)\n\n[The parts of a real composite] must give rise to **one quiddity and a single genuine unity**. As for dependence, it may run **in one direction**: as in a body composed of the simple elements, or in things constituted through mineral, vegetal and animal forms, where the form is said to need its matter without the converse dependence being exactly the same. Or dependence may be **reciprocal**, as in the relation reported between prime matter and form: matter needs form because its persistence is through form, and form needs matter insofar as its individualization takes place through matter. This is how the position is stated by its proponents.\n\n**“Unlike the conceptual composite.”** Here one cannot properly say that an extramentally existing collective configuration requires its material parts in the same manner, for the alleged collective form is simply an act of intellectual consideration. [The distinction between this thought-dependent unity and a real composition is elaborated by the marginal commentator.]\n\n### Original Arabic anchors (not full transcription)\n\nماهية واحدة وحدة حقيقية ؛ قد يكون من جانب واحد ؛ كالمركب من البسائط العنصرية ؛ الهيولى والصورة ؛ بخلاف الاعتباري ؛ محض اعتبار العقل\n\n### Separate lower-register commentary — selected source renderings and interpretive explanations, author unassigned\n\nThe lower note presses the apparent paradox: if a unity of composition is *merely considered* by the mind, it does not thereby become a second extramental thing over and above the parts. Yet this cannot be taken to deny every genuine composite—for example, one still has to explain whether a body's unity comes from substantial form and how its constituents depend on one another. The writer distinguishes an actual component from something brought into the analysis by stipulation; the same mereological language does not establish an identical mode of composition in each case. [Further objections to how a real form requires matter and whether the whole exists beyond its parts remain to be translated.]\n\n**Source-and-completeness caution:** The lower printed passage is longer than the selected English here; unresolved clauses and page joins are not silently filled. It remains a separately authored stream and is not a completed ḥāshiya translation.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":76,"body":"— Why a merely considered collective does not subsist; is the quiddity itself made?\n\n*Original printed Vol. I:76 · primary PDF physical page 89 · source-inspected first-pass text · not independently proofread.*\n\n### Upper printed commentary (Taftāzānī lemmata + Sanandajī, mixed)\n\n[A conceptual collective] has no realization as an additional thing in the external world. For an army, outside the mind, consists of the individual soldiers; if the collective configuration were counted as a further constituent of the army, the army would thereby have an element that does not actually exist outside the mind, and one would be driven to absurd conclusions about its external existence.\n\nThere is then a disagreement over **possible quiddities**: **are they made (majʿūla) by an agent, or are they not?** What is meant by being made must first be determined. If it means **needing an efficient cause**, the possible existent plainly needs a cause. But is this dependence something belonging to the *quiddity as such*, or does it follow upon its existence? The commentator reports the dispute and the preference expressed by the author of the base text. The issue must not be settled by taking “making” as though it meant merely adding an already existing quiddity to a further independent entity. [The specific alternatives continue on I:77.]\n\n### Original Arabic anchors (not full transcription)\n\nلا تحقق لها في الخارج ؛ إذ ليس من العسكر في الخارج إلا تلك الأفراد ؛ ثم إنهم اختلفوا في الماهيات الممكنة ؛ أهي مجعولة ؛ الاحتياج إلى الفاعل ؛ من لوازم الوجود\n\n### Separate lower-register commentary — selected source renderings and interpretive explanations, author unassigned\n\nThe lower gloss explicitly cautions that *being-made* can mean more than one thing. One sense concerns the thing's need for an agent; another concerns whether the quiddity itself is something for which an agent must supply being. A possible essence cannot be treated as though it subsisted by itself and then subsequently received existence without an argument establishing that separation. The writer also distinguishes real compounds—whose unity may require a substantial formal principle—from a conceptual group such as an army, whose abstract collectivity has no independent counterpart beyond the soldiers. [The ensuing detailed reply to the quiddity objection remains unfinished.]\n\n**Source-and-completeness caution:** The lower printed passage is longer than the selected English here; unresolved clauses and page joins are not silently filled. It remains a separately authored stream and is not a completed ḥāshiya translation.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":77,"body":"— Being made and causal dependence: quiddity versus existence\n\n*Original printed Vol. I:77 · primary PDF physical page 90 · source-inspected first-pass text · not independently proofread.*\n\n### Upper printed commentary (Taftāzānī lemmata + Sanandajī, mixed)\n\nThe termination of a body's magnitude is a concomitant of its *existing bodily state*: even if one supposes a body unlimited in magnitude, it remains a body. It is **not** a concomitant of quiddity in the way that **the evenness of four** follows the quiddity of four; if four were deprived of evenness it would no longer be four.\n\n**“Otherwise”**—if the intended meaning of the parties in dispute is not the distinction just explained—**“the possible's dependence upon its cause is necessary.”** To that extent, on the proposed interpretation, the controversy would be verbal. With this the exposition ends the discussions of **existence and quiddity** and turns to their attendant questions. [A new chapter begins on I:78.]\n\n### Original Arabic anchors (not full transcription)\n\nكتناهي الأجسام ؛ لا من لوازم الماهية كزوجية الأربعة ؛ وإلا ؛ فإن احتياج الممكن إلى العلة ضروري ؛ فلنفرغ عن مباحث الوجود والماهية\n\n### Separate lower-register commentary — selected source renderings and interpretive explanations, author unassigned\n\nThe marginal writer challenges the claim that dependence on an agent is wholly uncontroversial. The word *made* may refer to what is brought about, while dependence is a different relation requiring specification of the relevant subject. To say “the quiddity needs the agent” may mean that **an existent of that quiddity** needs an efficient cause, not that a quiddity considered independently of being must first be a completed object to which existence is attached. Thus the choice of meaning can make much of the disagreement verbal without erasing the real question of the ontological subject of causation. [The larger dispute is not exhausted by this translated selection.]\n\n**Source-and-completeness caution:** The lower printed passage is longer than the selected English here; unresolved clauses and page joins are not silently filled. It remains a separately authored stream and is not a completed ḥāshiya translation.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":78,"body":"— New chapter: individuation is not interchangeable with existence or quiddity\n\n*Original printed Vol. I:78 · primary PDF physical page 91 · source-inspected first-pass text · not independently proofread.*\n\n### Upper printed commentary (Taftāzānī lemmata + Sanandajī, mixed)\n\n**Chapter: On individuation (*al-taʿayyun*).** As stated in the *Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid*, individuation is other than a thing's **quiddity, existence and unity**, since each of these may be shared by it and another thing, whereas individuation is not shared in that manner. Thus it is correct to predicate of a universal that it has a quiddity, that it is existent [in the relevant sense], and that it is one; it is not correct to assert in the same sense that it is an individuated item. If individuation meant nothing other than those common descriptions, the difference in predication would be unexplained. [The reasoning continues with a disputed inference and a marginal challenge.]\n\n### Original Arabic anchors (not full transcription)\n\nفصل في التعين ؛ غير ماهيته ووجوده ووحدته ؛ لكون كل من هذه الأمور مشتركا بينه وبين غيره ؛ بخلاف التعين\n\n### Separate lower-register commentary — selected source renderings and interpretive explanations, author unassigned\n\nThe marginal commentary objects that a common predicate and a genuinely shared individual determination are not the same. Each of several individual existents may be called *existent*, while what makes each this individual is not, for that reason, common to them. The proof needs to distinguish a shared *notion of existence* from the specific existence of one individual, and the shared notion of *unity* from numerical individuation. Otherwise the reasoning risks treating two different senses of “common” as equivalent. [The remaining discussion of the relation between a mental notion and extramental individuation is untranslated.]\n\n**Source-and-completeness caution:** The lower printed passage is longer than the selected English here; unresolved clauses and page joins are not silently filled. It remains a separately authored stream and is not a completed ḥāshiya translation.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":79,"body":"— Whether individuation is something positive, privative or merely considered\n\n*Original printed Vol. I:79 · primary PDF physical page 92 · source-inspected first-pass text · not independently proofread.*\n\n### Upper printed commentary (Taftāzānī lemmata + Sanandajī, mixed)\n\nOne may predicate of a plurality the notion of individuation, since each individual has its own individuating state; yet this does not entail that the **very same individuation** belongs to all. For the individual determinations differ, even when the individuals share a quiddity. Likewise, *being distinct from another* can be predicated in a manner not identical with the thing's intrinsic individuation. Common predication of a word is not automatically identity of its concrete referent.\n\nAccordingly, a further disagreement arises: **Is individuation something existent (wujūdī) or privative (ʿadamī)? Is it real (ḥaqīqī) or a matter of consideration (iʿtibārī)?** Before adjudicating these positions, one must clarify what the respective terms mean. **“The individuals of a kind ...”** [The formulation of the alternatives continues on I:80.]\n\n### Original Arabic anchors (not full transcription)\n\nاختلف في أن التعين وجودي أو عدمي ؛ حقيقي أو اعتباري ؛ أفراد النوع\n\n### Separate lower-register commentary — selected source renderings and interpretive explanations, author unassigned\n\nThe lower writer urges that to describe individuation as a special *accident* attaching to a quiddity is insufficient by itself. A thing's being distinct from all others may be compared with a relation, and an absolute or relational predicate can still be said of many. The actual debate is whether, in addition to the quiddity and its existence, a distinct individuating entity must be posited. [The long marginal critique and citation of *Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid* have not been fully translated.]\n\n**Source-and-completeness caution:** The lower printed passage is longer than the selected English here; unresolved clauses and page joins are not silently filled. It remains a separately authored stream and is not a completed ḥāshiya translation.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":80,"body":"— The options: existence, nonexistence, accidents, and individuation\n\n*Original printed Vol. I:80 · primary PDF physical page 93 · source-inspected first-pass text · not independently proofread.*\n\n### Upper printed commentary (Taftāzānī lemmata + Sanandajī, mixed)\n\n[Continuing the dispute over individuation.] The individuating determination must give the individual a character that prevents its being shared. This may be explained as **“the individuating reality or individuation”**, understood as a determination accompanying the quiddity, or as the quiddity's **concomitant accidents**. Yet the question remains what kind of thing such a determination is.\n\n**“Or [something privative]”**: one possible analysis makes individuation an absence, either a simple absence or a relative absence, such as the exclusion of participation. **“Or additional to that”**—a combination of a positive and a negative description might be proposed. The wording asks whether each proposed determinant prevents sharing simply by being predicated of one thing or whether the very refusal of sharing is part of what it means to individuate.\n\n**“Or [an individuating state] existential”**: it might be asserted that individuation coincides with the individual's very external existence, or with something attached to that existence. Alternatively it may be taken as a **mental or conceptual consideration**, and not another independent extramental accident. But if it is only a consideration, there is still the fact that the individual itself is not in reality shared among many. The several possibilities are put forward as disputed alternatives, not as Sanandajī's unqualified commitments.\n\nThe following argument asks whether, on any of these alternatives, the quiddity can acquire individuation merely through a condition that would itself admit further predication. [The result continues on I:81.]\n\n### Original Arabic anchors (not full transcription)\n\nالتعين وهو تلك الهاذية ؛ الحاصلة بالعوارض ؛ أو ما فقدها ؛ أو عدم قبوله لها ؛ الوجودي ؛ الاعتباري ؛ لا يسمى أن التعين وجودي أو عدمي حقيقي أو اعتباري\n\n### Separate lower-register commentary — selected source renderings and interpretive explanations, author unassigned\n\nThe marginal critic distinguishes a *negative* account of individuation, which excludes another individual's sharing this very occurrence, from a *positive* constituent that exists independently. The denial of another's sharing does not, by itself, prove that a further real form must inhere in the subject. Likewise the conceptual act by which the intellect singles something out is not necessarily the same as the thing's being a single subject externally. [The remaining analysis of causes and accidents is still uncollated.]\n\n**Source-and-completeness caution:** The lower printed passage is longer than the selected English here; unresolved clauses and page joins are not silently filled. It remains a separately authored stream and is not a completed ḥāshiya translation.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":81,"body":"— Can a universal conception ever guarantee individual singularity?\n\n*Original printed Vol. I:81 · primary PDF physical page 94 · source-inspected first-pass text · not independently proofread.*\n\n### Upper printed commentary (Taftāzānī lemmata + Sanandajī, mixed)\n\nA further qualification concerns explanation by negation or conjunction. If one appends an additional description to a universal concept, the intellect can still conceive that fuller description as predicable of many. The same holds however many such conceptual descriptions are accumulated; their conjunction does not of itself necessitate that the resulting concept signify one and only one individual.\n\nThus individuation may be assigned **“to the free and powerful agent”**—this is the position presented as consonant with the author's account of all possible things depending ultimately upon God's free action. Or it may be assigned **“to external existence”**, as reported from some philosophers, on the ground that a quiddity which has become existent outside the intellect cannot be shared as an individual occurrence, whereas the quiddity conceived in the mind can.\n\nAn objection is raised. If existence is one in meaning, would one and the same cause of individuation not produce one and the same individuating result? The reply distinguishes unity of the **notion** of existence from the plurality of its **individual instances**, differentiated through times, places and the other conditions or causes. It is further noted that mere concomitance does not establish a causal explanation. The next objection is this: we apprehend an existent as an individual even while disregarding the additional conditions just mentioned. [The response opens I:82.]\n\n### Original Arabic anchors (not full transcription)\n\nلا يمكن للعقل فرض صدقه على كثيرين ؛ إما استنادا إلى الفاعل القادر ؛ أو إلى الوجود الخارجي ؛ وإنما تقبلها في العقل ؛ الوجود وإن كان بحسب المفهوم واحدا ؛ تتعدد أفراده بحسب الأزمنة والأمكنة وسائر الأسباب\n\n### Separate lower-register commentary — selected source renderings and interpretive explanations, author unassigned\n\nThe marginal writer distinguishes **the impossibility of actual sharing** of an external individual from the intellect's ability to form a repeatable *concept* of a similar individual. That a common concept can apply to many is not the same as many individuals participating in numerically the same concrete existence. If the alleged determinant is only “existence” without qualification, the problem recurs, for the concept of existence is itself common. Either the relevant determinant must be specified, or the argument must be understood as concerning actual individual occurrences and not the bare universal notion. [This paraphrase follows a selected stretch of the lower objection, which has not been translated completely.]\n\n**Source-and-completeness caution:** The lower printed passage is longer than the selected English here; unresolved clauses and page joins are not silently filled. It remains a separately authored stream and is not a completed ḥāshiya translation.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":82,"body":"— Individuality and the alternative of self-individuating quiddities\n\n*Original printed Vol. I:82 · primary PDF physical page 95 · source-inspected first-pass text · not independently proofread.*\n\n### Upper printed commentary (Taftāzānī lemmata + Sanandajī, mixed)\n\n**“We reply: setting a thing aside in thought does not entail its nonexistence.”** Thus, the fact that one recognizes the individuality of an external thing while not consciously considering its various accompanying causes does not show that those causes are absent.\n\n**“Or [individuation may be assigned] to other causes.”** This is given as the philosophers' position in cases where individuation is said to follow **“from the very quiddity”**. Then a kind may be confined to a single individual, the individuation following from the quiddity so understood; another individuation of that same quiddity would be impossible, and it would not be separable from the individuating condition already said to follow it.\n\nBut if individuation is taken to be in every case something **additional to the quiddity**, difficulties arise about how the extra item itself is individuated and how the explanation avoids further regress. Conversely, if a quiddity is said to be individuated **in itself**, the next enquiry is what is thereby meant by a nature's being individuated and whether a second individual can be conceived. [The sentence breaks at the end of I:82 and continues on I:83.]\n\n### Original Arabic anchors (not full transcription)\n\nقطع النظر عن الشيء لا يوجب انتفاءه ؛ أو إلى أسباب آخر ؛ نفس الماهية ؛ تنحصر النوع في الشخص الواحد\n\n### Separate lower-register commentary — selected source renderings and interpretive explanations, author unassigned\n\nThe marginal text says that an individual existent can have a particular cause or principle of individuation without the intellect attending to it in every act of judgment. The dispute is not resolved by simply pointing to the empirical fact that the thing is one. It is also necessary to distinguish cases where a quiddity admits numerous individuals from the purported case of a species whose very nature allows only one. This last question must not be confused with the claim that all quiddities have only one exemplar. [The rest of the lower leaf remains untranslated.]\n\n**Source-and-completeness caution:** The lower printed passage is longer than the selected English here; unresolved clauses and page joins are not silently filled. It remains a separately authored stream and is not a completed ḥāshiya translation.\n\n\n\n\n## Historical Stage I:55–68 accounting (retained unchanged; superseded by I:69–82)\n\n- **New original source positions directly inspected:** 14, printed I:55–68; physical PDF pages 68–81 inclusive. The original upper material has a new first-pass English rendering at each of these positions, with uncertain joins explicitly indicated. Parts of I:58–60 and I:66–68 need a second close source reading.\n- **Lower register:** only selected phrases and preliminary interpretive renderings are present; most of the smaller-type Arabic texts are **not** translated. Do not count lower English word totals as translated source-word coverage. The 14 leaves therefore **cannot be counted as 14 completed all-register translations**. Additional source voices have not been assigned to Wasīm without positive evidence.\n- **Old cumulative master:** the previous work I:2–54 is preserved, not replaced; this continuation is appended once to create one master I:2–68.\n- **Source and translation QA:** all 14 original source leaves are extracted unchanged from the verified original scan; page-number crosswalk and SHA-256 of primary scan are checked. No independent philological certification is asserted.\n- **Next original page to read:** I:69, continuing the nature, individuality and part/whole argument. The untranslated lower notes in I:55–68 remain a separate concrete backlog.\n\n\n## Current completion accounting after I:82\n\n---\n\n## Audit and remaining work\n\n- **Original positions inspected:** 14, I:69–82. Upper new-text stream exists on 13 leaves; I:73 is a lower-continuation-only page. None of these leaves is a completed all-register translation.\n\n- **Primary source:** `taqribmaram(2).pdf`, 632 physical page images, SHA-256 `4addd660de68794f9d64e062e3b25a2782497b3342eb4580218c3e07e8d12134`. Volume I printed p maps to physical page p+13.\n\n- **Lower material:** Only selected connected passages or explicit interpretive renderings are included. A full diplomatic Arabic transcription, lower-note boundary census and a complete translation of these notes remain to be done.\n\n- **Next contiguous primary-source page:** I:83 (continuation of the individuation argument); do not lose the lower marginal backlog from I:69–82.\n\n\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":83,"body":"— Self-individuating substances and individuation through successive dispositions\n\n*Printed I:83 · physical PDF 96 · first-pass mixed-upper and selected unassigned lower*\n\n### Upper printed argument (Taftāzānī lemma and Sanandajī mixed)\n\n[The philosophers assign individuation] **“either to the very individuated substance.”** In the case of the celestial intelligences, according to the position reported here, every separate substance is individuated through itself and thereby distinguished from every other. **“Or through the accidents accompanying it”**—in particular, **“through the succession of aptitudes.”** An example is the prime matter of the four elements. On the reported account this matter is a single common underlying principle, while the determinations distinguishing its occurrences follow the accidents that attach to it as its aptitudes succeed one another, depending upon nearness to or remoteness from the [celestial] sphere. These are alternatives advanced in the philosophical account, not a single homogeneous cause of individuation.\n\n### Selected Arabic anchors (not a transcription)\n\nأو إلى المادة المشخّصة ؛ بما يلحقها من العوارض ؛ بحسب تعاقب الاستعدادات ؛ كهيولى العناصر الأربعة\n\n### Lower register — selected source rendering / interpretation, writer unassigned\n\nThe lower gloss objects that the claim of a separate substance's self-individuation needs more than an assertion that the substance cannot admit another copy. If there is a common quiddity applicable to several substances, the question of what differentiates them returns. If there is instead a special quiddity for each, the dispute has shifted to whether such specifically distinct entities may nonetheless share one intelligible nature. The gloss also challenges the account of one matter successively individuated by differing accidents: if matter's individuation required those accidents, can the same individuated material subject be presupposed as the bearer of them? [The detailed cases remain untranslated.]\n\n**Completeness warning:** Significant lower-printed text remains untranslated; the English is not a diplomatic or independently proofread translation of the entire page.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":84,"body":"— New chapter: necessity, impossibility and possibility\n\n*Printed I:84 · physical PDF 97 · first-pass mixed-upper and selected unassigned lower*\n\n### Upper printed argument (Taftāzānī lemma and Sanandajī mixed)\n\n**Chapter: On necessity, impossibility and possibility.** These are among the determinations that attach to a quiddity when it is considered in relation to existence. They are compared to predications such as eternity and temporal origination. **“They are intelligibles”**—concepts formed by the intellect from the relation of a simple notion to its obtaining existence in itself, or to its not obtaining it. For when the intellect directs a concept toward existence, the result of this comparison falls under one of the three descriptions: *necessary*, *impossible*, or *possible*.\n\nThe author introduces the cases by way of examples. The [Necessary Existent's] existence is necessary; the existence of an impossible combination, such as the conjunction of contradictories, is impossible; and the existence of an ordinary contingent individual, such as a human being, is possible. The text then states that **“these three matters are necessary”**—that is, they are so immediately known that they do not stand in need of proper definition or demonstration in the usual way. They are defined, if at all, through their oppositions: necessity by reference to existence, impossibility by reference to nonexistence, and possibility by reference to the absence of the two necessities. [The last phrase is completed on I:85.]\n\n### Selected Arabic anchors (not a transcription)\n\nفصل في بيان الوجوب والامتناع والإمكان ؛ هي من الأمور العارضة للماهية ؛ معقولات ؛ تحصل من نسبة المفهوم إلى الوجود ؛ هذه الأمور الثلاثة ؛ لا تحتاج إلى تعريف\n\n### Lower register — selected source rendering / interpretation, writer unassigned\n\nThe lower writer distinguishes a quiddity considered simply in itself from that same quiddity when compared with external existence. “Necessity” is not an additional material part of a quiddity. Whether it is the existence of the object or its necessary relation to existence that is being considered must be stated. Otherwise, describing a quiddity's existence as necessary risks confusing the object of the judgment with the mental relation introduced by the intellect. [The remaining explanation of the three modal predicates is not translated in full.]\n\n**Completeness warning:** Significant lower-printed text remains untranslated; the English is not a diplomatic or independently proofread translation of the entire page.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":85,"body":"— What “necessary” and “impossible” mean; intrinsic and extrinsic modality\n\n*Printed I:85 · physical PDF 98 · first-pass mixed-upper and selected unassigned lower*\n\n### Upper printed argument (Taftāzānī lemma and Sanandajī mixed)\n\n[The terms are not offered as strict real definitions but as distinctions] **“of their expressions”** (*lafẓan*), meaning explanations of the words employed. Their meanings are already apparent to the intellect; one must not mistake the verbal explanation for a demonstration of a previously unknown essence.\n\n**“The first two are each divided”**—necessity and impossibility, not possibility—**“into intrinsic and extrinsic.”** Necessity of a thing's existence and necessity of its nonexistence may be considered either from the thing itself or in relation to something else. For example, the Necessary Existent's existence and the impossibility of a conjunction of contradictories are treated as intrinsic. Similarly, evenness belongs necessarily to four, whereas oddness is excluded from it. The impossibility of the negation of one of these properties is not simply the occurrence of an external preventing cause.\n\nBut the same division is also employed when existence or nonexistence is necessary **through another**. The source thus distinguishes a necessity arising from what the subject is in itself from one following upon a specified extrinsic condition or cause. [The remaining case division is carried forward to I:86.]\n\n### Selected Arabic anchors (not a transcription)\n\nلفظا ؛ ينقسم كل من الأولين ؛ إلى الذاتي والغيري ؛ وجود الباري ؛ عدم اجتماع النقيضين ؛ زوجية الأربعة\n\n### Lower register — selected source rendering / interpretation, writer unassigned\n\nOn the claim that the three modalities are self-evident, the gloss insists that the difference between knowing a general notion and understanding every technical restriction placed on it is real. An explanatory definition may help separate what is necessary *in itself* from what becomes necessary under a condition. The fact that the bare notions are familiar does not license moving without argument from intrinsic necessity to necessity through another. [The extended objection comparing the absence of opposites with an extrinsic condition remains uncollated.]\n\n**Completeness warning:** Significant lower-printed text remains untranslated; the English is not a diplomatic or independently proofread translation of the entire page.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":86,"body":"— Necessary through itself and through another; impossible through itself and through another\n\n*Printed I:86 · physical PDF 99 · first-pass mixed-upper and selected unassigned lower*\n\n### Upper printed argument (Taftāzānī lemma and Sanandajī mixed)\n\n**“Necessary through itself”** means what is characterized by intrinsic necessity of existence, such that the quiddity under the relevant consideration does not admit nonexistence. The example cited is God—exalted be He. **“Necessary through another”** refers to a thing whose existence follows necessarily through something distinct from it. The text invokes the evenness of four as an example of what follows necessarily from the relevant subject.\n\n**“Impossible through itself”** applies to what intrinsic contradiction excludes from existence, such as the conjunction of contradictories. **“Impossible through another”** is considered when the subject's existence is precluded by an external factor or the failure of a required condition. Accordingly, a thing's being prevented from existence need not mean that its own quiddity is contradictory. The distinction depends on whether the necessity or impossibility belongs to what is considered in itself or follows upon what is other than it.\n\nThe passage then introduces a qualification: what is called necessary through another does not cease on that account to be contingent in itself. [The contrast is completed on I:87.]\n\n### Selected Arabic anchors (not a transcription)\n\nواجب الوجود لذاته ؛ وهو الله تعالى ؛ لشيء آخر ؛ كزوجية الأربعة ؛ من الممتنع ؛ الموضوع والمحمول\n\n### Lower register — selected source rendering / interpretation, writer unassigned\n\nA separately ruled lower note remarks that a particular instance of impossibility may be bound up with a necessary relation other than a formal contradiction in the subject's own quiddity. Where a predication is prevented by an added condition, the word “impossible” must not be silently read as meaning intrinsically contradictory. The note also questions whether a simple quiddity can be “necessary through itself” in the same sense in which a proposition containing subject and predicate is necessary. [The larger lower argument remains untranslated.]\n\n**Completeness warning:** Significant lower-printed text remains untranslated; the English is not a diplomatic or independently proofread translation of the entire page.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":87,"body":"— How necessity and impossibility through another can be predicated of a possible thing\n\n*Printed I:87 · physical PDF 100 · first-pass mixed-upper and selected unassigned lower*\n\n### Upper printed argument (Taftāzānī lemma and Sanandajī mixed)\n\nA thing can be **necessary through another** while being *possible in itself*; it can also be **impossible through another** while its quiddity is not itself contradictory. Thus an external condition can necessitate the presence of an otherwise possible thing, while an opposing condition can prevent its presence. The possibility of the thing as considered in itself is not thereby removed.\n\n**“It is possible in the special sense”** when the two intrinsic necessities—of existence and of nonexistence—are both negated. More generally, possibility can signify that a specified necessity has been excluded, even where the opposite necessity is not thereby excluded. A term of modality must consequently be tied to the intended scope and the respect in which the thing is being considered. [The distinction between the special and general senses is pursued on I:88–89.]\n\n### Selected Arabic anchors (not a transcription)\n\nممتنع الوجود من عدمه ؛ الإمكان ؛ سلب ضرورة الوجود ؛ سلب ضرورة العدم ؛ الإمكان الخاص ؛ الوجوب والامتناع\n\n### Lower register — selected source rendering / interpretation, writer unassigned\n\nThe lower gloss points out that, when necessity through another is in question, the predicated necessity concerns a *relation under a condition*, whereas the nature considered simply in itself may remain contingent. The alleged contradiction between possible and necessary arises only if the two predicates are asserted of the same thing in precisely the same respect. Similarly, to deny necessity of existence is not yet to affirm intrinsic necessity of nonexistence. [The later elaboration of the general and special modal senses is not completely translated here.]\n\n**Completeness warning:** Significant lower-printed text remains untranslated; the English is not a diplomatic or independently proofread translation of the entire page.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":88,"body":"— Possibility in respect of a future occurrence: the short upper lemma\n\n*Printed I:88 · physical PDF 101 · first-pass mixed-upper and selected unassigned lower*\n\n### Upper printed argument (Taftāzānī lemma and Sanandajī mixed)\n\n[The source introduces another use of] **“possibility in respect of the future”** (*al-imkān bi-l-naẓar ilā l-istiqbāl*), meaning that the thing's existence **may occur in the future**. [The remainder of this leaf is a lengthy lower discussion of how this future-oriented sense is related to the special and general senses of possibility; there is no additional upper exposition.]\n\n### Selected Arabic anchors (not a transcription)\n\nالإمكان ؛ بالنظر إلى الاستقبال ؛ بمعنى جواز وجود الشيء في المستقبل\n\n### Lower register — selected source rendering / interpretation, writer unassigned\n\nThe marginal writer questions whether merely saying that something “may occur in the future” can be a genuine explication of possibility. A future event might, as a matter of its complete cause, be necessary, while what is impossible in itself does not become possible by shifting attention to a later time. The discussion therefore distinguishes the **absence of a known necessity** from the **actual absence of necessity**. To say that the future has not yet been settled for us is not an adequate definition of an objective modal status. The writer also observes that the general notion of possibility is not restricted to futurity: one can speak of possibility with regard to a past or present state, provided the intended modal sense is clear. [This selected argumentative rendering does not exhaust the long lower page.]\n\n**Completeness warning:** Significant lower-printed text remains untranslated; the English is not a diplomatic or independently proofread translation of the entire page.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":89,"body":"— Future possibility and dispositional possibility (*imkān istiʿdādī*)\n\n*Printed I:89 · physical PDF 102 · first-pass mixed-upper and selected unassigned lower*\n\n### Upper printed argument (Taftāzānī lemma and Sanandajī mixed)\n\nA future-oriented possibility is distinguished from possibility considered with respect to past or present occurrences. The future is not yet apprehended as having one of the two opposed states, existence or nonexistence. By contrast, in the past and the present one of those states is already actual in the relevant respect. The discussion then introduces **dispositional possibility** (*al-imkān al-istiʿdādī*).\n\nThis latter possibility is counted among **accidental qualities** associated with the preparation of a subject for receiving a form or effect. It is realized in differing degrees of intensity or weakness according to the subject's nearness to or remoteness from the conditions required for the effect. The familiar example is the successive aptitude for human development: what is present in the initial generative material is transformed through later embryonic stages. This is not the bare logical possibility of the human quiddity, for that remains considered in relation to the nature itself. Rather, this aptitude comes into being when some of the requisite conditions are present and ceases or is transformed when the effect previously present only in potency is actualized. [The source's closing attribution continues on I:90.]\n\n### Selected Arabic anchors (not a transcription)\n\nالاستقبال ؛ الإنسانية الحاصل للنطفة ثم للعلقة ثم للمضغة ؛ الإمكان الاستعدادي ؛ عرض ؛ مقولة الكيف\n\n### Lower register — selected source rendering / interpretation, writer unassigned\n\nThe marginal criticism develops a problem in the future-oriented definition. If the possibility of future existence is taken to require an absence of an already actual state, it seems to involve the present absence of the effect rather than an account of the future itself. If it is taken to require only that the effect may be produced in the future, that is already a modal premise and does not explain the concept. The gloss therefore separates the *conditions of a subject's preparation* from the modal status of the outcome, while retaining the possibility that a preparatory state can become stronger and eventually be replaced by the effect. [Other lines remain untranslated.]\n\n**Completeness warning:** Significant lower-printed text remains untranslated; the English is not a diplomatic or independently proofread translation of the entire page.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":90,"body":"— The material subject of preparedness; does a new event require pre-existing matter?\n\n*Printed I:90 · physical PDF 103 · first-pass mixed-upper and selected unassigned lower*\n\n### Upper printed argument (Taftāzānī lemma and Sanandajī mixed)\n\n[Continuing the discussion of readiness for an effect:] it is argued that **matter must precede** an originated thing, for if that matter itself were originated and required a prior matter in the same manner, an infinite regress would seem to follow. Matter is therefore claimed to be **“ancient”** in the relevant sense, serving as the **subject of possibility**. The argument proceeds: before an event exists it is possible, neither intrinsically impossible nor necessary; this possibility is taken to have a real, accidental status and hence to require a subject. That subject cannot be the future event itself, since the event does not yet exist, and cannot be a thing wholly external to it if the potentiality in question is precisely the event's potential existence. It is therefore located in the underlying matter.\n\nThe text then adds that the occurrence of actual effects depends upon the successive availability of conditions and causes **“in that matter”**. The proposal is a reported metaphysical argument for a material subject of preparedness, not an independent proof that an eternal matter exists. [The extended marginal refutation, including objections concerning origination, preparedness and regress, remains to be translated in full.]\n\n### Selected Arabic anchors (not a transcription)\n\nقديمة ؛ تكون تلك المادة ؛ محلا للإمكان ؛ الحادث قبل وجوده ممكن ؛ على المادة من الشرائط والأسباب\n\n### Lower register — selected source rendering / interpretation, writer unassigned\n\nThe lower commentary challenges the inference from the actuality of an effect's possibility to the necessary existence of a material substratum prior to it. “Possible” can be predicated of an effect considered in itself without thereby establishing a separately existing accidental quality that inheres in matter. Even where one posits an aptitude that genuinely belongs to a material subject, it does not follow that *every* possible thing must have a preparatory material subject in that sense. Otherwise the account risks assuming the very materialist premise it purports to prove. The margin also discusses whether an originated event requires the origination of matter, whether matter's capacity can persist without the effect and what kind of regress would follow if every potentiality needed a prior potentiality. [Only a selected part of this extended rebuttal is rendered here.]\n\n**Completeness warning:** Significant lower-printed text remains untranslated; the English is not a diplomatic or independently proofread translation of the entire page.\n\n\n---\n\n# New continuation: Volume I:91–104\n\nThe following fourteen positions are new source-inspected English with the authorial registers kept distinct. Uncertain clauses have not been silently completed, and long uncollated marginal arguments remain explicitly incomplete.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":91,"body":"— The possible thing and its need for an external determinant\n*Original printed Volume I:91 · physical source PDF 104 · provisional mixed upper; lower separately marked; no independent proof.*\n\n### Upper register — source-inspected working English\n\n[Continuing the argument from I:90 about a material subject of possibility.] The appeal to *preparedness* (*imkān istiʿdādī*) does not establish the stated conclusion without qualification. It must not be confused with the intrinsic possibility that belongs to a possible quiddity: the latter is opposed to both intrinsic necessity of existence and intrinsic impossibility, accompanies the possible nature under consideration, and is not absent simply because the subject has not yet come to exist. Nor does this intrinsic possibility, merely as such, entail an already existing material locus.\n\nNext comes the judgment that **a possible thing depends upon an efficient determinant with respect to its existence and its nonexistence**. The claim is grounded in the equality of its two sides considered from the nature itself. If neither existence nor nonexistence follows from what the thing is, the priority of one side over the other needs explanation. The author calls it necessary that **one of two equal sides cannot be preferred without a preferrer** (*tarjīḥ aḥad al-ṭarafayn bilā murajjiḥ*). The word “necessary” belongs to the immediately apprehended status of this judgment, not to the intrinsic necessity of the possible thing. [The sentence continues at the beginning of I:92.]\n\n### Arabic anchors (selected words; not a full transcription)\n\nالإمكان الاستعدادي ؛ الإمكان الذاتي اللازم للماهية الممكنة ؛ احتياج الممكن في وجوده وعدمه إلى المؤثر ؛ امتناع ترجيح أحد طرفيه بلا مرجح\n\n### Lower register — separate writer unassigned\n\n**Lower-register argument — first connected selection, writer unassigned.** The objection notes that the opponent himself recognizes a possible thing's possibility before its existence. This possibility is said to attach necessarily to the quiddity, yet the opponent also claims that it requires an existing locus, the matter of the possible thing. Thus, the bare assertion that a determination belongs to the quiddity is not enough to establish that it requires no existent locus. If the aim is to deny such a locus to intrinsic possibility, the more pertinent response is that **the possibility in question is an intellectual consideration (*amr iʿtibārī*)**, not an independently existing accident requiring a corporeal subject. This is different from treating *material preparedness* as a real disposition inhering in matter.\n\nThe lower writer then examines an argument that one of two sides of a possible thing must be specified by something external. A condition that belongs to the possible thing itself cannot simply be assumed to be such an externally selecting cause. If each side of the possible is treated as genuinely symmetrical, the relevant argument must establish what breaks the symmetry rather than merely giving that break a new name. [The remaining lower discussion of two equal sides continues onto I:92; its final clauses are not yet translated.]\n\n**Reading caution:** Opening words have been read in conjunction with I:90; a few compressed connectors and the last upper half-line need independent Arabic collation. Lower-note ending remains incomplete.\n\n**Coverage warning:** No complete all-register translation is claimed for I:91.\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":92,"body":"— Why the possibility of existence and nonexistence entails dependence\n*Original printed Volume I:92 · physical source PDF 105 · provisional mixed upper; lower separately marked; no independent proof.*\n\n### Upper register — source-inspected working English\n\n[Continuation:] One apprehends the judgment without needing to begin with a separate demonstration: a possible thing, when considered **in itself**, does not require either its existence or its nonexistence. **“Needing”** an external cause means precisely that neither of those two sides obtains through the thing's own essence; each, when it obtains, is accounted for by something beyond that essence.\n\nThere is, nevertheless, a challenge here. Could the occurrence of a possible thing be neither through itself nor through an external determinant, but simply occur **by coincidence**? The author draws attention to the apparent self-evidence of rejecting this alternative. Yet a claim's appearing obvious at first sight is not identical with a complete proof; the passage is preparing to test the force of the prohibition on preferring without a preferrer.\n\n**“And this”**—namely, the preference just mentioned and said to be inadmissible—must be distinguished from the selection of an alternative by a genuinely choosing agent. [The example of that selection begins on I:93.]\n\n### Arabic anchors (selected words; not a full transcription)\n\nلا يقتضي ذاته وجوده ولا عدمه ؛ معنى الاحتياج أن كلا من وجوده وعدمه لا يكون لذاته بل لأمر خارج ؛ بمجرد الاتفاق ؛ الترجيح\n\n### Lower register — separate writer unassigned\n\n**Lower-register selection — a reply concerning equal roads.** Suppose a fleeing person must choose one of two routes. It does not follow that there must be an independently determining motive in favor of the particular road taken. If such a motive were required, and if the agent were moved necessarily by that motive, the supposed freedom to take either road would disappear. The preference of **taking a route rather than remaining still** can be explained by the desire to escape, while the selection of **this route rather than that route** need not be attributed to a distinct attractive feature of the chosen road. To confuse the two questions is to make the supposed example prove more than it does.\n\nThe lower writer also distinguishes (i) the possibility of a thing considered in itself from (ii) its existence after all conditions for an actual occurrence are present. A thing's being intrinsically possible does not, simply by itself, establish an equal chance of its two alternatives under every specified set of conditions. [The long note continues into the following leaf.]\n\n**Reading caution:** The gloss reports and critiques several distinct attempts to reconcile freely choosing between equal alternatives with the maxim against preference without a preferrer. These are not one uncontested authorial assertion.\n\n**Coverage warning:** No complete all-register translation is claimed for I:92.\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":93,"body":"— The fleeing person and two equally effective paths\n*Original printed Volume I:93 · physical source PDF 106 · provisional mixed upper; lower separately marked; no independent proof.*\n\n### Upper register — source-inspected working English\n\n[The objection invokes] **the preference of one of two equal alternatives without a specifying factor**. The example is a person fleeing a wild beast: the person takes **one of two roads**, both equally capable of leading to the desired end, namely escape and safety. This is offered as an instance of choosing between alternatives even when there is no prior difference that requires one road rather than the other.\n\nThe author replies that **we do not declare such a choice impossible**, much less hold its impossibility to be an immediately necessary truth. Nor does its admissibility imply the admissibility of the preference that was rejected. In the present example, **the act of fleeing has already been preferred to not fleeing**. The agent's will selects one road and thereby leaves the other untaken. The motive of safety explains acting rather than remaining inactive, even if no separate motive attracts the agent to this particular route rather than to its equal. The agent's will can thus determine one option without a further difference in the roads themselves.\n\n**“If it is said ...”** The following objection turns to whether the preferred side must obtain in a way that would make the causal action redundant. [The opening of the objection is carried forward at I:94.]\n\n### Arabic anchors (selected words; not a full transcription)\n\nترجيح اختيار أحد المتساويين على الآخر بلا المخصص ؛ كالهارب من السبع ؛ يسلك أحد الطريقين المتساويين في الإفضاء إلى المطلوب الذي هو النجاة ؛ فنحن لا نقول بامتناعه ؛ فإن الهارب بإرادته يرجح سلوك أحد الطريقين\n\n### Lower register — separate writer unassigned\n\n**Lower margin — source-dependent argumentative summary, not a diplomatic translation.** The printed gloss presses the distinction between a motive explaining why the subject acts and a determination explaining why one of two outwardly indistinguishable means is chosen. It considers whether the will as such can function as a determining factor without a distinct desirable property attaching to one alternative. The remaining examples and replies in the dense lower text have not been fully deciphered.\n\n**Reading caution:** Read “two roads equal in leading to safety” literally; do not replace with a generic picture of equally desirable objects. The word introducing the speaker's objection at the end is clear; its continuation crosses the page boundary.\n\n**Coverage warning:** No complete all-register translation is claimed for I:93.\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":94,"body":"— Making an effect occur versus obtaining what is already there\n*Original printed Volume I:94 · physical source PDF 107 · provisional mixed upper; lower separately marked; no independent proof.*\n\n### Upper register — source-inspected working English\n\n[The objection asks about the supposed influence of a cause upon one of the two contradictory sides.] If the thing already exists, a further production of that very existence would be **obtaining what has already been obtained** (*taḥṣīl al-ḥāṣil*). If it does not exist, how can the alleged cause act upon it so as to make it exist? The difficulty depends on how **causal influence** (*taʾthīr*) and **bringing about** (*ījād*) are understood.\n\nThe author distinguishes the act of bringing a thing into being from the incoherent proposal of giving existence again to something already existent under that same description. Nor must the relation between cause and effect be pictured as an alteration of an already existing object. The objection assumes that, if a cause acts, it must find its effect already present in a way that makes the act superfluous, or else act upon sheer nonbeing as though that nonbeing were a receptive subject. The response denies that these are exhaustive descriptions of causal origination.\n\nThe final point is that one must be careful about the meaning of an **occurring effect** and the mode in which its coming to exist is predicated. [The compressed concluding clause requires a second textual check.]\n\n### Arabic anchors (selected words; not a full transcription)\n\nالتأثير والإيجاد ؛ تحصيل الحاصل ؛ تحصيل آخر غير هذا التحصيل ؛ ويختار أن التأثير حال العدم\n\n### Lower register — separate writer unassigned\n\n**Lower register:** The long gloss discusses the moment at which causal influence is supposed to take place: while the effect is absent, while it is present, or at the boundary between the two. It also questions whether “production” is being defined by mere correlation or by a real act of a productive source. **Only this issue-locating description is supplied here; no full lower-page translation is claimed.**\n\n**Reading caution:** The upper text has a very compact series of alternatives; this English preserves their argumentative structure but does not claim a certified rendering of each compressed connector.\n\n**Coverage warning:** No complete all-register translation is claimed for I:94.\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":95,"body":"— The occurrence of an event is not the whole meaning of its need for a cause\n*Original printed Volume I:95 · physical source PDF 108 · provisional mixed upper; lower separately marked; no independent proof.*\n\n### Upper register — source-inspected working English\n\nThe contention that causal influence is impossible on the proposed conception of an **originated event** does not by itself establish that temporal origination is the ground of need. One must distinguish the claim that a thing **comes to be after not being** from the claim that it **is intrinsically such that neither existence nor nonexistence is necessary for it**.\n\nAn inference that relies on the occurrence of an event must not silently import what is already supplied by its possibility. Nor may the presence or absence of a cause be inferred merely by repeating a verbal description of origination. The discussion cautions against treating a causal determination as a second physical act added to the first production of the event.\n\nThe question is now formulated explicitly: **Is what makes a thing need a cause its possibility (*imkān*), or its temporal origination (*ḥudūth*)?** The positions on that question follow in the next section. [The final upper words cross to I:96.]\n\n### Arabic anchors (selected words; not a full transcription)\n\nالترجيح ؛ امتناع التأثير ؛ حدوث ؛ المحوج ؛ الإمكان ؛ الحدوث\n\n### Lower register — separate writer unassigned\n\n**Lower register:** A separately printed objection begins by asking whether an alleged origination must involve a cause acting upon nonexistence, and whether the same account of causal action must be given before, at, and after the event. The gloss then examines whether causal priority can be taken as purely explanatory without assuming a distinct temporal interval. These are selected points of the objection; the original lower clauses are not entirely translated.\n\n**Reading caution:** The upper source is compressed and its opening does not stand independently of I:94. Treat this as an uncertain connected reading until a second direct collation.\n\n**Coverage warning:** No complete all-register translation is claimed for I:95.\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":96,"body":"— Two accounts of the ground of causal dependence: possibility and origination\n*Original printed Volume I:96 · physical source PDF 109 · provisional mixed upper; lower separately marked; no independent proof.*\n\n### Upper register — source-inspected working English\n\n**“The ground of need is possibility or origination.”** There is disagreement concerning this: the philosophers, together with some theologians, take **possibility** (*imkān*) to be what makes a thing need its cause, whereas earlier theologians take **temporal origination** (*ḥudūth*) as the ground. **“Each has a direction”**—that is, each position has an argument to offer.\n\nThe first account runs as follows. When the intellect considers a thing as **not requiring existence and not requiring nonexistence through itself**, it judges that neither of those sides can obtain without an external factor. This is the meaning of the thing's need for a cause. That judgment can be made whether or not the intellect also considers the thing as preceded by nonexistence. It is therefore possibility, rather than being preceded by nonexistence, that figures in the explanatory ground.\n\nThe second account considers the subject in respect of its actual **coming into existence after not existing**. On this view the judgment that it requires an originator attaches to it as originated. The two modes of considering the thing must not be treated as though they were simply the same premise in different words. [The second argument and replies continue on I:97.]\n\n### Arabic anchors (selected words; not a full transcription)\n\nوالمحوج هو الإمكان أو الحدوث ؛ ذهب إلى الأول الحكماء وبعض المتكلمين وإلى الثاني قدماء المتكلمين ؛ ولكل وجهة ؛ كونه غير مقتض للوجود والعدم بالنظر إلى ذاته\n\n### Lower register — separate writer unassigned\n\n**Lower register — selected argumentative distinction only.** One question raised is whether the intellect's recognition of the equal relation of the possible quiddity to existence and nonexistence establishes an extramental cause, or merely identifies a conceptual need requiring further proof. The opposing account makes the coming-to-be of the effect central. The printed writer asks whether each side is covertly assuming the causal principle it sets out to explain. [Not a complete translation of the lower page.]\n\n**Reading caution:** The names of the schools reflect the reporting in this printed source; no independent historical verification of each reported attribution is asserted.\n\n**Coverage warning:** No complete all-register translation is claimed for I:96.\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":97,"body":"— Possibility before existence; the intellectual order of the proof\n*Original printed Volume I:97 · physical source PDF 110 · provisional mixed upper; lower separately marked; no independent proof.*\n\n### Upper register — source-inspected working English\n\nA quiddity and its existence can be described as **possible** before the quiddity actually comes to exist. Temporal origination, on the other hand, is not predicated of the quiddity or its existence in this way until the thing has entered existence. This is one reason for giving possibility explanatory priority over origination in the proof of causal need.\n\nThe source cites the *Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid* to clarify that such priority is **according to the intellect**. The point is not that the mind's successive considerations themselves cause the external dependence. Rather, when the intellect apprehends the possibility or origination of a thing, it judges that the thing needs another. The question is how the mind correctly orders the conditions of that judgment.\n\nThere remains an objection: if what is meant by dependence is that a possible thing is equally related to existence and nonexistence until something external specifies it, then one must show that the relevant equality belongs to it **in reality**, not merely that our concept of the thing does not specify one side. A conceptual distinction must not, without argument, be substituted for the extramental dependence sought in the proof. [The discussion continues across the next leaf.]\n\n### Arabic anchors (selected words; not a full transcription)\n\nوصف الماهية ووجودها بالإمكان قبل أن تتصف به ؛ وأما الحدوث فلا تتصف به الماهية ولا وجودها إلا حال كونها موجودة ؛ قال في شرح المقاصد ؛ إن العلية إنما هي بحسب العقل\n\n### Lower register — separate writer unassigned\n\n**Separate lower critique, selected passage in close English.** The lower writer insists on keeping distinct (1) the intellect's judgment that a thing is possible, (2) the thing's actual being brought into existence, and (3) the condition under which an object is truly dependent. If “need” is only an intellectual relation, the conclusion about a genuinely independent cause does not follow without qualification. If “need” is a real condition, the conceptual consideration of possibility cannot be its only explanation. The remainder of the long note tests this choice by reference to the case of the possible object before its existence and after it comes into existence. [Several clauses remain untranslated.]\n\n**Reading caution:** The upper passage juxtaposes the two senses of explanatory priority; the second half is more argumentative than a literal transcription and needs close reinspection.\n\n**Coverage warning:** No complete all-register translation is claimed for I:97.\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":98,"body":"— Why the proponents of possibility consider their proof stronger\n*Original printed Volume I:98 · physical source PDF 111 · provisional mixed upper; lower separately marked; no independent proof.*\n\n### Upper register — source-inspected working English\n\nAs for the **demonstration**, the argument of those who say that **possibility** is the ground of causal need appears more readily acceptable. Its force is that **dependence holds of the possible thing in itself**, even if one sets aside the particular judgment of a person making the inference. One must accordingly locate the explanation of this dependence in what differentiates the possible from the necessary and the impossible.\n\nThe possible is not like what is necessary through itself, nor like what is impossible through itself. The equality of its relation to the two sides calls for something that determines which side obtains. This is what the proponents of possibility mean by the need for a cause. On this reasoning, the existence of a possible thing cannot be taken to follow from the mere quiddity, while the absence of any determining condition is still supposed.\n\nThe upper comparison is careful: it is **possibility that displays the indication of dependence most directly**, not an assertion that every possible thing is already in an actual state of preparation for being made. [Its specific objections are developed on I:99.]\n\n### Arabic anchors (selected words; not a full transcription)\n\nوأما الإثبات فكلام القائلين بأن المحوج هو الإمكان أظهر بالقبول ؛ لأن الاحتياج ثابت للممكن في نفس الأمر ؛ مع قطع النظر عن حكم حاكم ؛ في الممكن دون الواجب والممتنع\n\n### Lower register — separate writer unassigned\n\n**Marginal qualification (source-checked short selection):** The lower writer questions the phrase “more apparent.” Showing that possibility is conceptually associated with need is different from showing that it is the real *cause of* that need. In particular, an argument from the general possible quiddity to actual causal dependence may be sound only if the possibility is taken in the precise sense excluding both intrinsic necessities. The remainder of the gloss critiques the use of necessity and possibility as if they were attributes inhering in a ready-existing substrate. [Untranslated clauses remain.]\n\n**Reading caution:** A short, separately ruled lower note interrupts the long marginal text on this page. Its precise boundary has not yet been established.\n\n**Coverage warning:** No complete all-register translation is claimed for I:98.\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":99,"body":"— A possible existent can be dependent even if supposed beginningless\n*Original printed Volume I:99 · physical source PDF 112 · provisional mixed upper; lower separately marked; no independent proof.*\n\n### Upper register — source-inspected working English\n\nThe *possibility* of a thing can be considered **without temporal origination**. Even were one to suppose the possible existent to be beginningless, it would not cease to require an external determining cause, because its quiddity still does not explain why it exists. The inference to dependence therefore does not rest exclusively on its having once been absent.\n\nFor this reason the author describes the argument of those who make possibility the ground of need as stronger or more immediately acceptable, reversing the order of preference in the presentation under discussion from the *Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid*. An objection is then reported: **if the ground of need is possibility, why would the possible leave its state of nonexistence through a cause?** If possibility persists whether it exists or not, the critic says, that condition alone does not seem to explain the occurrence of existence at one time rather than another.\n\nThe reply distinguishes the general fact that the possible **needs another** from the particular determination that **existence rather than nonexistence is produced**. The first is traced to its possibility; the second involves the causal determination of the relevant side. A cause is not made redundant merely because possibility as such continues to belong to the thing. [The objection and its answer lead into I:100's account of different temporal senses of need.]\n\n### Arabic anchors (selected words; not a full transcription)\n\nالإمكان بدون الحدوث لاحتياج الممكن إلى مرجح خارج وإن فرض قدمه ؛ المحوج هو الإمكان ؛ خروج حالة العدم ؛ فيقدم تحصيل الحاصل بحصول سابق\n\n### Lower register — separate writer unassigned\n\n**Unassigned lower writer — only selected terms and argument so far:** The gloss considers whether possibility considered generally can explain *when* the possible exists and whether positing an eternal possible cause entails an eternal effect. It distinguishes the reality of the possibility that is being predicated from the additional determination of one of its sides. The long text has not yet been translated clause by clause.\n\n**Reading caution:** The last Arabic phrase of the objection is compressed in this scan. English brackets the inferred argument instead of asserting a certain reading for every intervening word.\n\n**Coverage warning:** No complete all-register translation is claimed for I:99.\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":100,"body":"— Need at origination and need during continued existence\n*Original printed Volume I:100 · physical source PDF 113 · provisional mixed upper; lower separately marked; no independent proof.*\n\n### Upper register — source-inspected working English\n\nThe author's question now concerns **the time at which an effect needs an efficient cause**. Does the need concern the occurrence of existence **initially**, the cessation of nonexistence, or the effect's **continuing to exist**? Alternatively, can “need” mean its dependence on the continued presence of a relevant condition or the absence of an opposing one?\n\nThe point of the distinction is not to assert a further existence added to the existence of the effect. **“Existence”** and **“nonexistence”** are the two relevant sides; one may consider the condition for bringing a thing from nonexistence into existence, and one may separately ask what it is to persist once existence has been obtained. The question whether the continuing thing needs its cause must be stated in a way that does not demand that the original production be repeated on every occasion as though it had never occurred.\n\nIf need **during persistence** is explained as the dependence of the current state upon what maintains or determines it, that is not the same as describing a new production after prior nonexistence. The text insists on keeping the senses separate before drawing an inference about the possibility and origination accounts. [The statement continues into I:101.]\n\n### Arabic anchors (selected words; not a full transcription)\n\nمعنى الاحتياج إلى المؤثر ؛ إما وقت الوجود أو العدم ابتداء ؛ أو توقف استمرارهما حال البقاء على أمر ما ؛ وجود العلة وانتفاؤها ؛ حال المقابلين في البقاء\n\n### Lower register — separate writer unassigned\n\n**Lower text:** The note on this page distinguishes what is being said of **the cause** from what is being said of **the effect** at the point of origination and at the point of persistence. It asks whether the proposed expression “the time of need” names a real interval preceding an effect or a conceptual distinction among conditions. The full marginal examination has not been collated here.\n\n**Reading caution:** Printed I:100 is part of a continuous argument reaching I:101. The short alternatives in parentheses are translated provisionally, not all authorially disambiguated.\n\n**Coverage warning:** No complete all-register translation is claimed for I:100.\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":101,"body":"— Persistence and the two sides of the possible\n*Original printed Volume I:101 · physical source PDF 114 · provisional mixed upper; lower separately marked; no independent proof.*\n\n### Upper register — source-inspected working English\n\n[Continuing:] The alleged need at the state of persistence is explained by considering whether continued **existence** or continued **nonexistence** depends upon the relevant determining condition. The intended need is a need with respect to the state in question; it does not simply refer to a second origination of the thing after it has already come to exist.\n\nThe argument turns again to the general point that the **existence of the possible and its nonexistence are equal in relation to its essence**. If the intellect considers the possible without regard to external determination, neither side has intrinsic priority. The supposed preference of one side therefore calls for a determinant. This is the ground on which those who make *possibility* the cause of need argue, whether one considers initial occurrence or persistence.\n\n**“The claim”** under discussion may also be expressed by saying that the mind apprehends the possible as not intrinsically requiring existence or nonexistence, while the actual obtaining of one side demands something further. The text then asks how far this principle extends: must every possible side be produced by a further cause in exactly the same manner? [The ensuing distinction appears at I:102.]\n\n### Arabic anchors (selected words; not a full transcription)\n\nمعنى الاحتياج حال البقاء إما توقف الوجود أو العدم المفروض في الحال ؛ وجود الممكن وعدمه بالنظر إلى ذاته سواء ؛ الإمكان ؛ سلب ضرورة الوجود\n\n### Lower register — separate writer unassigned\n\n**Marginal selection (not complete):** The distinction between an individual positive effect and the negation of that effect creates a special problem. If both sides of the possible are said to need causes in the same sense, one may seem to require an efficient producer even of nonexistence. The gloss seeks to distinguish a cause that produces an actuality from an explanatory condition under which an absence obtains. More follows in the original lower text.\n\n**Reading caution:** Main text refers back to the two senses of the causal need proposed on I:100. Technical clauses in the second half require independent verification.\n\n**Coverage warning:** No complete all-register translation is claimed for I:101.\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":102,"body":"— The absence of intrinsic priority, and selection of one side\n*Original printed Volume I:102 · physical source PDF 115 · provisional mixed upper; lower separately marked; no independent proof.*\n\n### Upper register — source-inspected working English\n\n**“The absence of intrinsic priority”** means that neither side of a possible thing obtains through the essence itself. If the side of existence is to be preferred, something must give it priority; likewise, the absence of existence does not become intrinsic necessity merely because the thing has not occurred. But the author's argument distinguishes what is known through **the bare possibility** from what is additionally required to specify **a particular event or its time**.\n\nThere can be a distinction between the thing's being possible in the absolute sense and its possible **occurrence at a given time**. The latter involves a specified instance of the side of existence; to explain that instance one considers the conditions by which the effect is actually brought about. The presence of those conditions does not turn the quiddity, considered simply in itself, into something intrinsically necessary.\n\nThe following objection asks whether a preference at a particular time can be admitted **without an independent external specifier**. If such preference is allowed, will it not conflict with the earlier maxim against preferring one equal side without a preferrer? The response develops by distinguishing an original condition from a fresh, successive cause. [Continued I:103.]\n\n### Arabic anchors (selected words; not a full transcription)\n\nانتفاء الأولوية الذاتية ؛ كون الطرف الذي له الأولوية طرف آخر ؛ يكون بسبب مرجح ؛ وقوع الطرف الأول موقوفا على عدم ذلك السبب ؛ لا يكون أولى بالنظر إلى ذات الممكن\n\n### Lower register — separate writer unassigned\n\n**Lower commentary:** The gloss asks whether absolute possibility and temporal preparedness are being conflated once more. It distinguishes the mere logical non-necessity of an event from the additional readiness of a particular subject to receive it under the requisite conditions. The source writer questions whether invoking the general modal relation, by itself, explains a temporally determinate occurrence. [This is a selected argumentative account; many lines remain untranslated.]\n\n**Reading caution:** Some Arabic is compressed around the parenthesized arguments. Exact wording of the temporal case remains open to re-reading.\n\n**Coverage warning:** No complete all-register translation is claimed for I:102.\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":103,"body":"— Why a further preferrer cannot always be demanded\n*Original printed Volume I:103 · physical source PDF 116 · provisional mixed upper; lower separately marked; no independent proof.*\n\n### Upper register — source-inspected working English\n\n[The first alternative:] if the preference of one side over the other took place **without an external cause**, one might say that the side had priority solely through the essence. But a preference of this sort cannot simply be asserted, since the subject is the possible thing whose nature, by supposition, is indifferent between the two sides.\n\nOn the other alternative, a **further determining factor** is demanded for the preference itself. The author considers the threat of a regress if every determining factor must in turn be made to occur by another, distinct factor in precisely the same way. This demands care over what is being counted as an effect and what as the determining relation. It does not suffice to declare the occurrence of a side inexplicable merely by saying that the other side was previously absent.\n\nThe printed argument distinguishes what belongs to the thing in itself from the way a cause specifies its existence **at a particular time**, and from what merely presupposes its prior nonexistence. **Earlier nonexistence**, in itself, is not a positive efficient agent. [The concluding contrast concerning the absence of a previously existing side passes to I:104.]\n\n### Arabic anchors (selected words; not a full transcription)\n\nالذات أحد الطرفين دون الآخر ؛ لا بد من انتهاء إلى حد الوجود ؛ أمر خارج عن الذات ؛ امتناع الترجيح بلا مرجح ؛ وقبل العدم أولى بالأعراض السالبة\n\n### Lower register — separate writer unassigned\n\n**Lower register:** The writer distinguishes between an actual causal source and an attributed “priority” that may only be a relation between the two sides of a judgment. A further condition is not automatically a distinct, additional efficient cause, and therefore the alleged regress has to be formulated with precision. This records the dispute's central question, but it is not a complete translation of the long lower passage.\n\n**Reading caution:** Technical sentence concerning the first alternative is partially damaged/compressed; this working English is not certified word for word.\n\n**Coverage warning:** No complete all-register translation is claimed for I:103.\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":104,"body":"— Temporal beginning versus the general causal need of the possible\n*Original printed Volume I:104 · physical source PDF 117 · provisional mixed upper; lower separately marked; no independent proof.*\n\n### Upper register — source-inspected working English\n\n[Continuing:] the argument uses the **distinction between the occurrence of existence and its absence**, but neither time nor a prior absence should be taken as though it were itself an efficient cause. An assertion of an event's not existing at one time and existing at another must be distinguished from an explanation of why the relevant side is specified.\n\nThe commentator considers the need for another as arising from the possible thing's **not requiring either side through itself**. The side of existence is possible, and its absence is likewise not excluded by the bare quiddity. If one asks whether the effect needs the cause only **at its first occurrence** or also while it continues, the answer depends on whether one means initial production or dependence more generally.\n\nIn this debate the expression **“need for another”** must not be assumed to mean only movement out of a preceding state of nonexistence. On the other hand, if one asserts a continuing need while refusing every real determination beyond the quiddity, the assertion risks leaving unexplained how the one side obtains rather than the other.\n\nThe last printed contrast is drawn between a thing's **not being able to exist without the cause** and the separate claim that a cause must produce a *new* beginning at each moment. The next source leaf, I:105, completes the argument by relating this to the impossibility of preference without a preferrer. [I:105 is preserved as following context and not counted as translated here.]\n\n### Arabic anchors (selected words; not a full transcription)\n\nوجود الممكن محفوف بوجودين ؛ أحدهما الأول سابق على وجوده ؛ والثاني لاحق له ؛ انتفاء الأولوية الذاتية ؛ ما يجب\n\n### Lower register — separate writer unassigned\n\n**Lower register:** The separate note scrutinizes an argument that treats a first existence and the absence preceding it as distinct explanatory entities. The question is whether the purported plurality belongs to the world or only to our analysis of an event's temporal description. The full lower rebuttal has not been completed.\n\n**Reading caution:** I:104 is continuous with I:105; an uncertain bracket covers the compressed causal-status contrast, not a supplied ending absent from the source.\n\n**Coverage warning:** No complete all-register translation is claimed for I:104.\n\n\n\n---\n\n# Continuation: Volume I:105–110","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":105,"body":"— Could a specified event occur without a new preferrer?\n*Printed I:105 · physical PDF 118 · mixed upper first-pass source reading; lower separately identified; not independently certified.*\n\n### Upper — first-pass English\n\n[Continuing I:104:] The demand for an external determinant of one side does not mean that, whenever an actual effect occurs, the whole condition that makes it preferable must itself begin **at the same instant** as the effect. The text is opposing an argument which, from the absence of an additional preferrer at a specified time, infers the complete impossibility of the occurrence.\n\nIf neither of two sides had any priority at all, then the obtaining of one side rather than the other would indeed constitute the preference of an equal alternative **without any preferrer**. But the disputants disagree over what counts as the relevant priority. One view takes the continued specification supplied by a choosing cause to be enough; another argues that the occurrence of existence **at this moment rather than at another** demands an additional determinate condition. The absence of such a distinct new condition does not by itself prove that the act is without a preferrer.\n\nThe upper passage distinguishes the **absence of a fresh temporal cause** from the **absence of any determining factor**. It also warns against simply assuming the side of nonexistence has intrinsic priority and therefore making the transition to existence unintelligible by definition. [The line concluding the discussion of alternatives continues at I:106.]\n\n### Arabic anchors (not a full original transcription)\n\nلم يوجد امتناع الترجيح بلا مرجح ؛ اللازم على تقدير عدم الانتهاء إلى حد الوجود ؛ لاحد الطرفين أولوية ؛ المرجح\n\n### Lower — different historical writer; author not verified\n\n**Lower passage — a selected challenge, writer unassigned.** A need for a preferrer must be distinguished from a need for a newly originated preferrer. If a choosing agent can fix a particular time for an event, the argument cannot exclude that explanation merely by asking for another change immediately before the time chosen. Conversely, a temporal selection cannot be given explanatory force by merely renaming it “priority.” The printed writer develops further objections regarding whether the condition that selects one time is itself necessary or contingent; that continuous lower argument is not yet completely translated.\n\n**Reading caution:** The beginning and end of this leaf are mid-argument. Some source-internal connective references are only approximately rendered.\n\n**Completeness warning:** The whole printed lower register and certain upper clauses remain unfinished.\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":106,"body":"— A preference, and the impossibility of combining existence and nonexistence\n*Printed I:106 · physical PDF 119 · mixed upper first-pass source reading; lower separately identified; not independently certified.*\n\n### Upper — first-pass English\n\n[Continuing:] if one side is to obtain at its appointed time, the act of preferring it must not be portrayed as a preference that lacks a preferrer. The next argument refers to the **impossibility of joining the contradictory sides**, existence and nonexistence, in one subject under the same consideration.\n\n**“The choice”** is explained as the preference of the side brought about by a selecting agent. The author then distinguishes the impossibility of **simultaneous existence and nonexistence** from a demand that the possible entity, considered in itself, be intrinsically determined to one side. The choosing relation cannot be treated as if it were the same thing as the logical impossibility of a conjunction of contradictories.\n\nHe next turns back to **necessity, impossibility and possibility**, considered as kinds of modal description. They belong to the argument in different ways: a thing's possible quiddity can remain unchanged when external causes bring it into being, while a proposition about the actual occurrence at a given time may be necessary under a fully specified condition. [The discussion introduces definitions of what is *ancient* and what is *originated*, continued on I:107.]\n\n### Arabic anchors (not a full original transcription)\n\nامتناع الجمع بين الوجود والعدم ؛ وهذا الإنفاق الاختيار ؛ ثلاثة أي الوجوب والامتناع والإمكان ؛ بل كلما\n\n### Lower — different historical writer; author not verified\n\n**Lower passage not completely translated.** The margin distinguishes an external selector from the mere impossibility of asserting contradiction. One may not infer from the law of noncontradiction that an intrinsically possible thing has a naturally preferred side. The printed note also comments on the meaning of modal predicates as intellectual considerations. Remaining clauses await a direct source collation.\n\n**Reading caution:** The printed phrase concerning the choosing act and the first modal description is compressed; the English is an explicitly provisional connected rendering.\n\n**Completeness warning:** The whole printed lower register and certain upper clauses remain unfinished.\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":107,"body":"— Ancient and originated: a mode of being, not a separate species\n*Printed I:107 · physical PDF 120 · mixed upper first-pass source reading; lower separately identified; not independently certified.*\n\n### Upper — first-pass English\n\nThe text now considers the expressions **“ancient”** (*qadīm*) and **“originated”** (*ḥādith*). When a thing is described as ancient, this may be understood as something whose existence **has no beginning**. It is not thereby established that there must be an additional independently existing reality called ancientness attached to it. Nor does every use of the word make what is ancient a distinct species of being.\n\nLikewise, an originated thing is described in relation to the **nonexistence preceding its existence**. The author draws attention to different usages of the words and the scope of the description. A thing can be described as ancient or originated **according to its own essence** or under a condition relating it to another; the two classifications must not be confused.\n\nThe discussion also warns against converting **ancientness** into a positive, separable attribute whose subject must first be found. What is being asserted may only be the denial of a beginning. **“Origination”**, conversely, can designate the event's obtaining existence after its absence. [The next page develops the relations between these predicates and the subject's quiddity.]\n\n### Arabic anchors (not a full original transcription)\n\nوصفا أي قديم ؛ عمومه ؛ كالقدم ؛ قدم الذات ؛ والحدوث ؛ وجود الحادث ؛ الموصوف بالقدم\n\n### Lower — different historical writer; author not verified\n\n**Marginal note:** One objection asks whether, if “ancient” and “originated” apply to existence, they are being taken as constitutive types or merely predicates of an existent under the relevant temporal relation. The printed discussion treats individuation and universal predication separately from the denial or affirmation of a temporal beginning. [Only the central disputed distinction is recorded here.]\n\n**Reading caution:** The text distinguishes a universal description from a concrete existent; part of the printed line on the first usage is not fully resolved.\n\n**Completeness warning:** The whole printed lower register and certain upper clauses remain unfinished.\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":108,"body":"— Predication, rational consideration, and whether ancientness is a positive property\n*Printed I:108 · physical PDF 121 · mixed upper first-pass source reading; lower separately identified; not independently certified.*\n\n### Upper — first-pass English\n\n[The argument continues:] if **ancientness** were something positive, existing alongside its subject as a distinct accompaniment, it would itself require an explanation of its own status. One would have to ask whether that accompaniment was likewise ancient and whether an endless sequence of predicates or accompanying realities followed. A negative meaning such as **“not preceded by nonexistence”** does not by itself require such an independently subsisting extra item.\n\nSimilarly, the description of a subject as **characterized by** ancientness or origination must be distinguished from the supposition that these attributes are additional existences outside the intellect. Some relevant expressions designate **intellectual considerations** (*iʿtibārāt ʿaqliyya*), and their ascription to the thing need not imply an external accident inhering in it.\n\nIf the thing's **existence** is necessarily connected with a particular predicate, that still does not show that the predicate and existence are two separate positive realities. The author tests the distinction between what belongs to the reality itself and what comes from the mind's mode of considering its relation to existence or nonexistence. [The sentence concludes on I:109.]\n\n### Arabic anchors (not a full original transcription)\n\nوالتعين ؛ والموصوفية ؛ اعتبارات عقلية ؛ والأزلية ؛ في الأمور الوجودية ؛ لا يجوز أن يكون وجود الوجود\n\n### Lower — different historical writer; author not verified\n\n**Lower register:** A substantial note takes issue with multiplying existing attributes whenever a subject can correctly be described by several notions. It contrasts an objectively true predication with an additional component actually present in the object. The argument is incomplete here and cannot yet serve as a fully verified translation of the lower page.\n\n**Reading caution:** This is an issue-sensitive rendering of the upper reasoning, not a complete literal transcription of the dense parenthetical syntax.\n\n**Completeness warning:** The whole printed lower register and certain upper clauses remain unfinished.\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":109,"body":"— Different senses of priority: outside the mind and in the intellect\n*Printed I:109 · physical PDF 122 · mixed upper first-pass source reading; lower separately identified; not independently certified.*\n\n### Upper — first-pass English\n\nIf the description were attributed to the subject merely because we regard it in a certain way, one must distinguish that mental mode of comparison from what is **affirmed of the object outside the mind**. The passage asks whether **ancientness and origination** are states that obtain in external reality or whether the mind's consideration of before and after introduces the relevant distinction.\n\nThe author replies by distinguishing **the being of the subject in the external world** from the **conceptual form** by which the mind judges it. Something can truly be said to possess existence in the world although the concept of existence is formed in the mind. Conversely, simply conceiving it as having a particular temporal predicate does not give that predicate an independent external existence.\n\nThe resulting judgment must specify the intended sense of **“before”**. A priority of being is not interchangeable with a priority of the intellect's consideration, nor with priority according to time. The author returns to the terms **ancientness** and **origination**, and their application to the reality, to avoid treating their temporal meaning as a separable property. [The last phrase carries over to I:110.]\n\n### Arabic anchors (not a full original transcription)\n\nمعنى كون الذي واجبا ؛ في الخارج ؛ بحسب إذا عقل ؛ إلى الوجود ؛ الحاصل له قبل الأذهان ؛ القدم والحدوث\n\n### Lower — different historical writer; author not verified\n\n**Marginal register:** The gloss objects that a predication's being *true outside the mind* need not imply a positive extra constituent called “ancientness.” It also asks whether the time relation used to describe the thing existed before it was conceptualized, and what notion of priority is presupposed. The remainder of the dense argument has not been independently collated.\n\n**Reading caution:** Some words near the first division of senses are uncertain. Do not treat the language about external being as a separate modern metaphysical thesis.\n\n**Completeness warning:** The whole printed lower register and certain upper clauses remain unfinished.\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":110,"body":"— Intrinsic and temporal ancientness, and what “preceded by nonexistence” means\n*Printed I:110 · physical PDF 123 · mixed upper first-pass source reading; lower separately identified; not independently certified.*\n\n### Upper — first-pass English\n\nThere are two relevant senses of **ancientness**. **Intrinsic ancientness** is understood as not being preceded by nonexistence in a sense concerning the essence itself. **Temporal ancientness** means not having a temporal nonexistence before it. The distinction matters because a beginninglessness understood under one respect does not straightforwardly establish beginninglessness under another.\n\nLikewise, what is **originated** can be described by reference to a **preceding nonexistence**, but this expression admits an intrinsic and a temporal use. The *intrinsically originated* is that whose essence, considered in itself, does not entail existence. Temporal origination refers to nonexistence before the actual beginning of the thing. On this division, an event may be contingent in itself without its temporal starting point being the sole cause of its dependence.\n\nThe author notes that what is considered ancient or originated in these respects must not be defined simply by a number of elapsed years. Nor is the temporal sense to be confused with the philosophical assertion that every possible thing must have a measurable beginning. The terminology is deployed in the continuing dispute about whether a possibly beginningless created order would remain dependent upon a cause.\n\n[The last upper phrase introduces the contrast with the Muʿtazilites and continues on I:111. The remaining lower text on I:110 has not been translated in full.]\n\n### Arabic anchors (not a full original transcription)\n\nقديما وزمانيا ؛ القدم المسبوقية بالغير ؛ عدم المسبوقية بالعدم ؛ الحدوث ؛ بالعدم الزماني ؛ القدم الذاتي\n\n### Lower — different historical writer; author not verified\n\n**Lower text — partial selection only.** The marginal writer distinguishes a thing's intrinsic dependence upon its causal ground from being preceded by an interval of time. Something can have a cause without the explanation being a temporal sequence. The writer also questions the application of “nonexistence before existence” where the alleged before is not temporal. [Specific counterexamples and a long polemical sequence remain untranslated.]\n\n**Reading caution:** This division is a working source reading; the exact school attribution on the following page is deliberately not added before inspecting I:111.\n\n**Completeness warning:** The whole printed lower register and certain upper clauses remain unfinished.\n\n\n---\n\n**Historical completion point (preceding Stage I:111–116):** I:111. Marginal commentaries were then substantially unfinished.\n\n\n---\n\n# New direct-source, denser translation: I:111–116","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"}]);
